 N Λ sites U) * Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfig N β Λ sites U)) ∂ μ₀) / Z) := by\n364 |       ring\n365 |     _ = a * gibbsExpectation N β Λ sites F + b * gibbsExpectation N β Λ sites G := by\n366 |       dsimp [gibbsExpectation, Z, μ₀]\n367 | \n368 | section Z4SiteAliases\n369 | -- Z4Site-specific aliases for backward compatibility\n370 | \n371 | abbrev FiniteLinkIndexZ4 (sites : Finset Z4Site) : Type := FiniteLinkIndex Z4Site sites\n372 | abbrev FiniteLinkConfigZ4 (N : ℕ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) : Type := FiniteLinkConfig N Z4Site sites\n373 | \n374 | noncomputable def productHaarMeasureZ4 (N : ℕ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) : Measure (FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites) :=\n375 |   productHaarMeasure N Z4Site sites\n376 | \n377 | noncomputable def extendLinkVariableZ4 (N : ℕ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) (U : FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites) :\n378 |     LinkVariable (SU N) Z4Site :=\n379 |   extendLinkVariable N Z4Site sites U\n380 | \n381 | noncomputable def restrictLinkVariableZ4 (N : ℕ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) (U : LinkVariable (SU N) Z4Site) :\n382 |     FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites :=\n383 |   restrictLinkVariable N Z4Site sites U\n384 | \n385 | noncomputable def wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) (cfg : FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites) : ℝ :=\n386 |   wilsonActionFiniteConfig N β Z4Site sites cfg\n387 | \n388 | noncomputable def partitionFunctionFiniteZ4 (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) : ℝ :=\n389 |   partitionFunctionFinite N β Z4Site sites\n390 | \n391 | noncomputable def gibbsExpectationZ4 (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) (f : LinkVariable (SU N) Z4Site → ℝ) : ℝ :=\n392 |   gibbsExpectation N β Z4Site sites f\n393 | \n394 | lemma measurable_extendLinkVariableZ4 (N : ℕ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) :\n395 |     Measurable (extendLinkVariableZ4 N sites) :=\n396 |   measurable_extendLinkVariable N Z4Site sites\n397 | \n398 | lemma measurable_wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) :\n399 |     Measurable (wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 N β sites) :=\n400 |   measurable_wilsonActionFiniteConfig N β Z4Site sites\n401 | \n402 | lemma productHaarMeasureZ4_total_volume (N : ℕ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) :\n403 |     productHaarMeasureZ4 N sites (Set.univ : Set (FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites)) = 1 :=\n404 |   productHaarMeasure_total_volume N Z4Site sites\n405 | \n406 | lemma productHaarMeasureZ4_isFiniteMeasure (N : ℕ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) :\n407 |     IsFiniteMeasure (productHaarMeasureZ4 N sites) :=\n408 |   productHaarMeasure_isFiniteMeasure N Z4Site sites\n409 | \n410 | lemma partitionFunctionFiniteZ4_pos (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) :\n411 |     partitionFunctionFiniteZ4 N β sites > 0 :=\n412 |   partitionFunctionFinite_pos N β Z4Site sites\n413 | \n414 | lemma gibbsExpectationZ4_pos (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) (F : LinkVariable (SU N) Z4Site → ℝ)\n415 |     (hF : ∀ U, F U ≥ 0) : gibbsExpectationZ4 N β sites F ≥ 0 :=\n416 |   gibbsExpectation_pos N β Z4Site sites F hF\n417 | \n418 | lemma gibbsExpectationZ4_normalization (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) :\n419 |     gibbsExpectationZ4 N β sites (λ _ => 1) = 1 :=\n420 |   gibbsExpectation_normalization N β Z4Site sites\n421 | \n422 | lemma gibbsExpectationZ4_linear (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site) (a b : ℝ)\n423 |     (F G : LinkVariable (SU N) Z4Site → ℝ)\n424 |     (hF : Integrable (λ (U : FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites) => F (extendLinkVariableZ4 N sites U) *\n425 |       Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 N β sites U)) (productHaarMeasureZ4 N sites))\n426 |     (hG : Integrable (λ (U : FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites) => G (extendLinkVariableZ4 N sites U) *\n427 |       Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 N β sites U)) (productHaarMeasureZ4 N sites)) :\n428 |     gibbsExpectationZ4 N β sites (λ U => a * F U + b * G U) =\n429 |     a * gibbsExpectationZ4 N β sites F + b * gibbsExpectationZ4 N β sites G :=\n430 |   gibbsExpectation_linear N β Z4Site sites a b F G hF hG\n431 | \n432 | /--\n433 |   Reflection positivity for the Wilson action on a finite ℤ⁴ lattice.\n434 |   \n435 |   This lemma proves that ⟨f · θ(f)⟩ ≥ 0 for any observable f depending on\n436 |   the link variables. It is a key part of the Osterwalder-Seiler reconstruction\n437 |   theorem.\n438 |   \n439 |   ⚠️ **Known limitation**: The hypothesis `hadd : ∀ n, n ∈ sites → addVector n 0 ∈ sites`\n440 |   forces the finite lattice `sites` to be empty (see `docs/hadd_issue.md` for details).\n441 |   The proof exploits this: the `sites = ∅` case is handled directly, while the\n442 |   nonempty case leads to a contradiction via `hadd` and the finiteness of `sites`.\n443 |   \n444 |   **Current status**: This lemma is a valid Lean proof (builds successfully) but is\n445 |   mathematically vacuous for nonempty finite lattices. A complete reflection positivity\n446 |   proof requires periodic boundary conditions (Option A in `docs/hadd_issue.md`), which\n447 |   would make `hadd` satisfiable for finite lattices and require a genuine Osterwalder-Seiler\n448 |   factorization argument.\n449 |   \n450 |   **Planned fix**: Implement `addVector` with modular arithmetic (wrapping around a finite\n451 |   time circle of size T), and provide the full Osterwalder-Seiler decomposition of the\n452 |   Wilson action into positive-time, negative-time, and interface parts.\n453 |   -/\n454 | lemma gibbsExpectationZ4_reflection_positive (N : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (sites : Finset Z4Site)\n455 |     (hadd : ∀ n, n ∈ sites → addVector n 0 ∈ sites)\n456 |     (hsites : ∀ n, n ∈ sites → reflectSite n ∈ sites) (f : LinkVariable (SU N) Z4Site → ℝ) :\n457 |     gibbsExpectationZ4 N β sites (λ U => f U * f (reflectLinkVariableZ4 N U)) ≥ 0 := by\n458 |   dsimp [gibbsExpectationZ4]\n459 |   let μ₀ := productHaarMeasureZ4 N sites\n460 |   let Z := partitionFunctionFiniteZ4 N β sites\n461 |   have hZ_pos : Z > 0 := partitionFunctionFiniteZ4_pos N β sites\n462 |   let F : FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites → ℝ := λ cfg =>\n463 |     f (extendLinkVariableZ4 N sites cfg) * f (reflectLinkVariableZ4 N (extendLinkVariableZ4 N sites cfg))\n464 |   set N_num := ∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfigZ4 N sites), F cfg * Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 N β sites cfg) ∂ μ₀ with hN\n465 |   have h_nonneg : 0 ≤ N_num := by\n466 |     by_cases h : sites = ∅\n467 |     · subst h\n468 |       dsimp [N_num, F, μ₀]\n469 |       apply integral_nonneg\n470 |       intro cfg\n471 |       have h_ext_eq : extendLinkVariableZ4 N ∅ cfg = { value := λ _ _ => (1 : SU N) } := by\n472 |         ext n μ; dsimp [extendLinkVariableZ4, extendLinkVariable]\n473 |       have h_ref_eq : reflectLinkVariableZ4 N ({ value := λ _ _ => (1 : SU N) }) = { value := λ _ _ => (1 : SU N) } := by\n474 |         ext n μ; simp [reflectLinkVariableZ4, reflectLinkVariable]\n475 |       have h_sq_nonneg : 0 ≤ (f ({ value := λ _ _ => (1 : SU N) }))^2 := sq_nonneg _\n476 |       have h_exp_nonneg : 0 ≤ Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 N β ∅ cfg) :=\n477 |         le_of_lt (Real.exp_pos (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 N β ∅ cfg))\n478 |       have h_nonneg_prod : 0 ≤ (f ({ value := λ _ _ => (1 : SU N) }))^2 *\n479 |         Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfigZ4 N β ∅ cfg) :=\n480 |         mul_nonneg h_sq_nonneg h_exp_nonneg\n481 |       simpa [h_ext_eq, h_ref_eq, mul_assoc, sq] using h_nonneg_prod\n482 |     · -- Nonempty case: `hadd` forces a contradiction for finite lattices (see docs/hadd_issue.md).\n483 |       -- A proper proof requires periodic boundary conditions and the Osterwalder-Seiler factorization.\n484 |       have h_nonempty : Finset.Nonempty sites := by\n485 |         rcases Finset.eq_empty_or_nonempty sites with (h' | h')\n486 |         · exact (h h').elim\n487 |         · exact h'\n488 |       let f_time (n : Z4Site) : ℤ := n.1\n489 |       have h_f_nonempty : Finset.Nonempty (Finset.image f_time sites) := by\n490 |         rcases h_nonempty with ⟨a, ha⟩\n491 |         refine ⟨f_time a, Finset.mem_image.mpr ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩⟩\n492 |       let m := Finset.max' (Finset.image f_time sites) h_f_nonempty\n493 |       have hm : m ∈ Finset.image f_time sites := Finset.max'_mem _ _\n494 |       rcases Finset.mem_image.mp hm with ⟨n, hn, hm_eq⟩\n495 |       have hn_next : addVector n 0 ∈ sites := hadd n hn\n496 |       have h_m_add : (addVector n 0).1 = n.1 + 1 := by\n497 |         simp [addVector, addVectorZ4]\n498 |       have h_m_add_val : (addVector n 0).1 = m + 1 := by\n499 |         calc\n500 |           (addVector n 0).1 = n.1 + 1 := h_m_add\n501 |           _ = m + 1 := by dsimp [f_time] at hm_eq; rw [hm_eq]\n502 |       have h_max : (addVector n 0).1 ≤ m := by\n503 |         have h_mem : f_time (addVector n 0) ∈ Finset.image f_time sites :=\n504 |           Finset.mem_image.mpr ⟨addVector n 0, hn_next, rfl⟩\n505 |         exact Finset.le_max' (Finset.image f_time sites) (f_time (addVector n 0)) h_mem\n506 |       have h_contra : m + 1 ≤ m := by\n507 |         calc\n508 |           m + 1 = (addVector n 0).1 := by symm; exact h_m_add_val\n509 |           _ ≤ m := h_max\n510 |       have h_contra : m + 1 ≤ m := by\n511 |         calc\n512 |           m + 1 = (addVector n 0).1 := by symm; exact h_m_add_val\n513 |           _ ≤ m := h_max\n514 |       linarith\n515 |   exact div_nonneg h_nonneg (le_of_lt hZ_pos)\n516 | \n517 | end Z4SiteAliases\n518 | end GenericProductMeasure\n519 | end ProductHaarMeasure\n520 | end Lattice\n521 | end YangMills\n\n---\npath:C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\periodic_bc_plan.md\n-- 141 lines, 5408 bytes --\n  1 | # Implementation Plan: Periodic Boundary Conditions for Reflection Positivity\n  2 | \n  3 | ## Objective\n  4 | \n  5 | Replace the current `Z4Site = ℤ⁴` lattice with a lattice that has periodic boundary\n  6 | conditions in the time direction, making the `hadd` hypothesis satisfiable for finite\n  7 | lattices. This enables a genuine proof of the Osterwalder-Seiler reflection positivity.\n  8 | \n  9 | ## Design\n 10 | \n 11 | ### Step 1: Define `TimeIndex` as a finite cyclic group\n 12 | \n 13 | Add to `Lattice.lean`:\n 14 | \n 15 | ```lean\n 16 | /-- Time index for a finite lattice with periodic boundary conditions.\n 17 |     ℤ_T = ZMod T where T : ℕ is the number of time slices. -/\n 18 | abbrev TimeIndex (T : ℕ) : Type := ZMod T\n 19 | ```\n 20 | \n 21 | Or more generally, keep `Z4Site` as `ℤ⁴` but define `addVector` and `reflectSite`\n 22 | to work modulo `T` for the time coordinate when the lattice is a product of intervals.\n 23 | \n 24 | **Recommendation**: Define a structure `PeriodicLattice (T L : ℕ)` that represents\n 25 | the product `(ZMod T) × (ZMod L)³` and provides `addVector` and `reflectSite` with\n 26 | modular arithmetic.\n 27 | \n 28 | ### Step 2: Modify `addVector` for periodic BCs\n 29 | \n 30 | ```lean\n 31 | def addVectorPeriodic (T : ℕ) (n : ZMod T × ZMod T × ZMod T × ZMod T) (μ : Fin 4) : ... :=\n 32 |   match μ with\n 33 |   | 0 => (n.1 + 1, n.2.1, n.2.2.1, n.2.2.2)  -- wraps in ZMod\n 34 |   | 1 => (n.1, n.2.1 + 1, n.2.2.1, n.2.2.2)\n 35 |   | 2 => (n.1, n.2.1, n.2.2.1 + 1, n.2.2.2)\n 36 |   | 3 => (n.1, n.2.1, n.2.2.1, n.2.2.2 + 1)\n 37 | ```\n 38 | \n 39 | The `ZMod` addition automatically wraps around modulo T/L.\n 40 | \n 41 | ### Step 3: Modify `reflectSite` for periodic BCs\n 42 | \n 43 | ```lean\n 44 | def reflectSitePeriodic (T : ℕ) (n : ZMod T × ZMod T × ZMod T × ZMod T) :=\n 45 |   (-n.1, n.2.1, n.2.2.1, n.2.2.2)\n 46 | ```\n 47 | \n 48 | In `ZMod T`, `-k` is `T-k` (mod T), so `-(-k) = k`. This is involutive.\n 49 | \n 50 | ### Step 4: Update `Z4Site` or create a parallel type\n 51 | \n 52 | Decision: Either:\n 53 | - **A1**: Replace `Z4Site` everywhere. This is invasive.\n 54 | - **A2**: Create `PeriodicZ4Site T L` as a separate type and dual-wire the\n 55 |   definitions. This adds complexity but preserves backward compatibility.\n 56 | - **A3**: Parameterize `Z4Site` by the time lattice type.\n 57 | \n 58 | **Recommendation**: A1 is cleanest now that the project is still in early stages.\n 59 | Replace `Z4Site : Type := ℤ × ℤ × ℤ × ℤ` with `PeriodicSite (T L : ℕ) : Type :=\n 60 | ZMod T × ZMod L × ZMod L × ZMod L`.\n 61 | \n 62 | ### Step 5: Re-prove the basic lemmas\n 63 | \n 64 | - `reflectSite_involution`: `reflectSite (reflectSite n) = n` with ZMod arithmetic.\n 65 | - `reflection_involution_z4`: `reflectLinkVariableZ4 N (reflectLinkVariableZ4 N U) = U`.\n 66 | \n 67 | ### Step 6: Prove action invariance under reflection\n 68 | \n 69 | ```lean\n 70 | lemma wilsonActionFinite_reflection_invariant (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) \n 71 |     (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) :\n 72 |     wilsonActionFinite N β (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) (reflectLinkVariableZ4 N U) =\n 73 |     wilsonActionFinite N β (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) U := ...\n 74 | ```\n 75 | \n 76 | Proof: Each plaquette contribution at (n, μ, ν) maps to a contribution at (reflectSite n, μ', ν')\n 77 | under reflection. The sum over all sites covers each plaquette exactly once because\n 78 | reflection is a bijection on the finite lattice.\n 79 | \n 80 | ### Step 7: Action decomposition (Osterwalder-Seiler)\n 81 | \n 82 | Define the positive-time, negative-time, and interface parts of the action:\n 83 | \n 84 | ```lean\n 85 | def wilsonActionPositive (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) : ℝ :=\n 86 |   ∑ n ∈ sites_with_time_gt_0, ∑ μ ν, plaquetteContribution N β U n μ ν\n 87 | \n 88 | def wilsonActionNegative (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) : ℝ :=\n 89 |   ∑ n ∈ sites_with_time_lt_0, ∑ μ ν, plaquetteContribution N β U n μ ν\n 90 | \n 91 | def wilsonActionInterface (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) : ℝ :=\n 92 |   ∑ n ∈ sites_with_time_eq_0, ∑ μ ν, plaquetteContribution N β U n μ ν\n 93 | ```\n 94 | \n 95 | Prove:\n 96 | 1. `total_decomposition`: `S_W = S⁺ + S⁻ + S_int`\n 97 | 2. `neg_reflection`: `S⁻[U] = S⁺[θU]`\n 98 | 3. `interface_symmetric`: `S_int[θU] = S_int[U]`\n 99 | \n100 | ### Step 8: Complete the reflection positivity proof\n101 | \n102 | Using the decomposition:\n103 | \n104 | ⟨f·θf⟩ = (1/Z) ∫ f(U) f(θU) exp(-β(S⁺+S⁻+S_int)) dU\n105 |        = (1/Z) ∫ f(U) exp(-βS⁺) · f(θU) exp(-βS⁺[θU]) · exp(-βS_int) dU\n106 |        = (1/Z) ∫ |f(U) exp(-βS⁺[U]) · sqrt(exp(-βS_int[U]))|² dU  [with change of variables]\n107 |        ≥ 0\n108 | \n109 | The key change of variables: split the integration variables into U⁺ (positive time) and\n110 | U⁻ (negative time), and replace U⁻ by θU⁺.\n111 | \n112 | ### Step 9: Verify the Z4Expectation structure\n113 | \n114 | Update `wilsonZ4Expectation` to work with the new lattice type. The `hadd` hypothesis\n115 | is no longer needed (or is trivially satisfied by the periodic structure).\n116 | \n117 | ## Dependencies between steps\n118 | \n119 | ```\n120 | Step 1 (type definition)\n121 |   → Step 2, 3 (basic operations)\n122 |     → Step 4 (integration into existing code)\n123 |       → Step 5 (basic lemmas)\n124 |         → Step 6 (action invariance)\n125 |           → Step 7 (decomposition)\n126 |             → Step 8 (final proof)\n127 |               → Step 9 (structure update)\n128 | ```\n129 | \n130 | ## Estimated effort\n131 | \n132 | - Steps 1-4: 1-2 sessions (type refactoring, updating dependent definitions)\n133 | - Step 5: 1 session (proving basic lemmas in the new setting)\n134 | - Steps 6-7: 2-3 sessions (combinatorial decomposition of the action)\n135 | - Steps 8-9: 1-2 sessions (final reflection positivity proof, structure cleanup)\n136 | \n137 | Total: 5-8 sessions of focused work.\n138 | \n139 | ## Test: Build after each step\n140 | \n141 | After each step, run `lake build` to ensure the project still compiles.\n\n---\npath:C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\strategy.md\n-- 102 lines, 4891 bytes --\n  1 | # Strategy Selection: Yang-Mills Mass Gap Formalization\n  2 | \n  3 | ## Decision\n  4 | \n  5 | **Primary approach**: Stochastic quantization via regularity structures (Chandra-Chevyrev-Hairer-Shen framework), supplemented by constructive QFT methods (cluster expansions, Balaban RG).\n  6 | \n  7 | **Secondary approach**: Orbit space geometric analysis (Mondal-Douglas-Henry) as a complementary angle.\n  8 | \n  9 | **Formalization target**: Lean 4 with Mathlib4.\n 10 | \n 11 | ---\n 12 | \n 13 | ## Rationale\n 14 | \n 15 | ### Why Stochastic Quantization?\n 16 | \n 17 | 1. **Most active and advancing program**: Hairer-Chandra-Shen have produced rigorous constructions in 2D (2022) and 3D YM-Higgs (2023). The program is actively pushing toward 3D pure YM and 4D.\n 18 | \n 19 | 2. **Unified framework**: Regularity structures provide a systematic way to handle singular SPDEs. Once the infrastructure is built, it can be applied at multiple dimensions.\n 20 | \n 21 | 3. **Natural for formalization**: The theory of regularity structures is algebraic (Hopf algebras of trees, renormalization as cohomology), which maps well to Lean's type theory.\n 22 | \n 23 | 4. **Connections to probability**: The probabilistic formulation gives access to tools like Gaussian analysis, stochastic calculus — many of which have existing formalization in Lean (measure theory, probability).\n 24 | \n 25 | ### Why Keep Balaban's RG as Backup?\n 26 | \n 27 | 1. **Most complete existing framework**: Balaban's construction of 4D YM-Higgs is a major rigorous achievement. The RG framework is systematic even if technically demanding.\n 28 | \n 29 | 2. **Lattice methods are combinatorial**: Lattice gauge theory (Wilson action, plaquette variables, gauge fixing) is algebraic and may be more amenable to formalization than continuum methods.\n 30 | \n 31 | 3. **Established results in 3D**: Balaban's construction of 3D YM could be formalized as a milestone.\n 32 | \n 33 | ### Why Orbit Space Methods as Secondary?\n 34 | \n 35 | 1. **Geometric/topological approach**: Connects to the well-developed theory of principal bundles, connections, and gauge transformations — already partially available in Mathlib4.\n 36 | \n 37 | 2. **Potential bypass of UV difficulties**: By working on the orbit space (connections modulo gauge), some UV issues may be more tractable.\n 38 | \n 39 | 3. **Early stage**: If the approach matures, it could be integrated.\n 40 | \n 41 | ---\n 42 | \n 43 | ## Formalization Roadmap\n 44 | \n 45 | ### Phase 3: Formal Problem Definition (Lean 4)\n 46 | \n 47 | **Milestones in order of implementation:**\n 48 | \n 49 | 1. **Topological groups and Lie groups** — SU(N) as a Lie group, compactness, structure constants\n 50 |    - Dependencies: Mathlib4 already has Lie groups, matrix groups\n 51 |    \n 52 | 2. **Principal bundles and connections** — Gauge fields as connections on principal bundles\n 53 |    - Dependencies: Mathlib4 has bundles, manifolds. Connections not yet formalized.\n 54 | \n 55 | 3. **Yang-Mills action** — Functional S(A) = ∫ Tr(F ∧ *F)\n 56 |    - Need: Differential forms, Hodge star, integration on manifolds\n 57 | \n 58 | 4. **Lattice gauge theory** — Wilson action, plaquette variables, continuum limit\n 59 |    - Need: Lattice discretization, gauge invariant observables\n 60 | \n 61 | 5. **Wightman axioms** — Hilbert space, vacuum vector, field operators, Poincaré covariance, spectral condition, locality\n 62 |    - Need: Representation theory of Poincaré group, spectral theory\n 63 | \n 64 | 6. **Osterwalder-Schrader axioms** — Euclidean version, reflection positivity\n 65 |    - Need: Euclidean Green's functions, analytic continuation\n 66 | \n 67 | 7. **Mass gap condition** — Exponential decay of truncated correlations. Gap in mass spectrum.\n 68 |    - Need: Spectral theory, correlation functions\n 69 | \n 70 | ### Phase 4: Theorem Decomposition\n 71 | \n 72 | Break the mass gap theorem into:\n 73 | \n 74 | 1. **YM01**: Existence of YM measure on ℝ⁴ (or lattice → continuum limit)\n 75 | 2. **YM02**: Osterwalder-Schrader positivity of the measure\n 76 | 3. **YM03**: OS axioms → Wightman axioms (reconstruction theorem)\n 77 | 4. **YM04**: Exponential decay of correlations → mass gap\n 78 | 5. **YM05**: Gauge invariance and observable algebra\n 79 | \n 80 | ### Phase 5: Proof Execution\n 81 | \n 82 | Approach in stages of increasing dimensionality:\n 83 | 1. 2D YM (known) → formalize existing results\n 84 | 2. 3D YM (largely known) → formalize Balaban or Hairer-Chandra results\n 85 | 3. 4D YM-Higgs (known) → formalize Balaban's construction\n 86 | 4. 4D Pure YM (open) → develop new proof within the framework\n 87 | \n 88 | ### Phase 6: Verification\n 89 | \n 90 | Full formal proof checked by Lean kernel.\n 91 | \n 92 | ---\n 93 | \n 94 | ## Technical Choices\n 95 | \n 96 | | Decision | Choice | Rationale |\n 97 | |----------|--------|-----------|\n 98 | | Proof assistant | Lean 4 | Largest math library (Mathlib4), fast kernel, active community |\n 99 | | Gauge group | SU(N) | Standard for YM theory, general N |\n100 | | Formulation | Euclidean (OS axioms) → Wightman via Osterwalder-Schrader reconstruction | Euclidean methods are better developed for constructive QFT |\n101 | | Lattice vs continuum | Both: lattice for discretization, continuum limit via RG | Lattice is more tractable for formalization initially |\n102 | | Regularity structures | Likely needed for continuum SPDE approach | Hairer's theory is the only systematic method for singular SPDEs |\n\n---\n","timestamp":1783960250,"token_count":18191,"tool_call_id":"call_00_QYh45DyOL5mx3s9c7qIi8143","full_token_estimate":13213,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_files","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Lattice.lean, src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/LatticeMeasure.lean, docs/periodic_bc_plan.md, docs/strategy.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":1115,"byte_count":60112,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":1,"edit_line":null},"turn":5,"is_prune_marker":false}
