tially use the fact that the transfer matrix is a finite-rank operator (since L² of a compact group is separable but the space of functions on a FINITE set of links is finite-dimensional in some sense... actually no, SU(N) is infinite, so L²(SU(N)) is infinite-dimensional)\n\nWait, actually on a finite lattice with finitely many links, each link variable lives in SU(N) which is a compact Lie group. The space L²(SU(N)^(links)) is infinite-dimensional (since each SU(N) copy contributes infinite dimensions). So we can't reduce to finite dimensions.\n\nHowever, there's a special property: the interface action S_OS_int only involves a subset of the links (those crossing the time-0 interface). The transfer matrix T acts on L²(μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁰), and the key is to show T is positive.\n\nFor the Wilson action, S_OS_int is a sum of plaquette terms that each involve at most 4 link variables. The Boltzmann factor exp(-β S_OS_int) is a product of terms, each of which is a positive function on the configuration space.\n\n**Key insight**: Since the Haar measure on SU(N) is finite, and the Boltzmann factor is bounded (between 0 and 1), the transfer matrix T is a bounded operator. Its positivity follows from the fact that the integral kernel K(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = exp(-β S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) is a positive-definite function on SU(N)^(links).\n\nBut proving this kernel is positive-definite requires the Peter-Weyl theorem... or does it?\n\nThere might be a simpler argument: The Boltzmann factor is a product over plaquettes of terms of the form exp(β/N Re Tr(U_∂p)). Each term is a positive function on SU(N)⁴ (since the trace is bounded by N, and β > 0). The product of positive functions is positive. And any positive function on a compact group generates a positive integral operator...\n\nWait, this is getting into functional analysis. The statement \"if K is a positive function, then the integral operator with kernel K is positive\" is NOT true in general. It's only true if K is positive-definite in the sense of group theory, not just pointwise positive.\n\nSo the Peter-Weyl theorem IS needed to show that the kernel, when expanded in the representation basis, has positive coefficients (which is equivalent to the operator being positive).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. For the Wilson action on a finite lattice, there's a known result: the transfer matrix is a positive operator because the Boltzmann weight can be written as a sum of squares in the \"loop algebra\" — this is essentially the Osterwalder-Seiler argument.\n\nLet me look at what's been done more carefully. The proof is reduced to showing:\n\n∫ G(U) G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n\nwhere G(U) = f(U) exp(-β S_OS⁺(U)) exp(-β S_OS_int(U)/2).\n\nThe factorization gives:\n∫ G·θG dμ₀ = ∫_{U⁺,U⁰} ∫_{U⁻} G(U⁺,U⁰) G(U⁻,U⁰) exp(-β S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\n= ∫_{U⁺,U⁰} G(U⁺,U⁰) (T G)(U⁺,U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nwhere (Tψ)(U⁺,U⁰) = ∫ ψ(U⁻,U⁰) exp(-β S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nSo ∫ G·θG = ⟨G, T G⟩ ≥ 0 if T is a positive operator.\n\nFor the Wilson action, the interface kernel K(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰) = exp(-β S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)) can be expanded as a sum over representations of SU(N) (Peter-Weyl) with positive coefficients. This is because exp(-β S_OS_int) can be written as a product over plaquettes crossing the interface, each of which is a positive function on SU(N)⁴. The key is that for each such plaquette, the function can be written as ∑_λ c_λ Tr(ρ_λ(plaquette)) with c_λ ≥ 0 (by a theorem of Osterwalder-Seiler, essentially because the heat kernel on SU(N) is positive-definite... no, that's different).\n\nActually, the standard argument (OS 1979, §3) uses the fact that the Wilson action is reflection positive because it can be written as a sum over plaquettes, each of which is reflection-positive individually. The paper shows that for each plaquette crossing the interface, the contribution factorizes as a sum of squares in the group algebra.\n\nThis is getting deep. Let me reconsider what's practical:\n\nSince this is a formalization project with a documented gap, and the Peter-Weyl theorem is a well-understood piece of mathematics that IS the subject of active formalization efforts in Mathlib (there are PRs for representation theory of compact Lie groups), perhaps the best approach is:\n\n1. **Document the gap thoroughly** (already done)\n2. **Start working on Phase 7** in parallel — the continuum limit and mass gap proof\n3. **Monitor Mathlib for Peter-Weyl theorem availability**\n\nBut actually, let me look at whether we can make ANY progress on the Peter-Weyl front, or if there's a simpler approach specific to SU(N).\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what the user (next session) needs. The session briefing says:\n\n> To continue:\n> - The next session should focus on either:\n>   a. Formalizing the Peter-Weyl theorem for SU(N)\n>   b. Finding a simpler argument\n>   c. Leaving the gap and moving to Phase 7\n\nLet me explore what already exists in Mathlib for representation theory of compact Lie groups, and check if any relevant lemmas are available.\n\nBut wait — I don't have Lean or lake installed on this system, so I can't check Mathlib directly. Let me search the web for the current state of representation theory in Mathlib.\n\nActually, let me focus on what I CAN do. Let me think about whether there's a simpler argument for the finite lattice case.\n\nThe key observation is that for a finite periodic lattice, the configuration space is COMPACT (it's a finite product of SU(N) copies). On a compact space:\n- Continuous functions are bounded\n- The integral is finite\n- The transfer matrix is a compact operator on L²\n\nFor the specific case of the Wilson action on a finite lattice, the interaction is local and the Boltzmann factor is bounded and continuous. The transfer matrix T is an integral operator with kernel K(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = exp(-β S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)).\n\nNow, the function exp(-β S_OS_int) is positive (pointwise > 0). But more importantly, it's a product of terms of the form exp(β/N Re Tr(plaquette)), where each plaquette involves at most 4 SU(N) variables.\n\nThere might be a way to use the fact that each plaquette term is itself a positive-definite function on the appropriate SU(N)⁴, and the product of positive-definite functions is positive-definite. But this requires the notion of positive-definiteness on a group...\n\nActually, let me look at this from a different angle. For a finite system, we could potentially use a spectral argument:\n\n1. The transfer matrix T is a bounded self-adjoint operator on L²\n2. The heat kernel on SU(N) is positive (by standard harmonic analysis)\n3. For small β, the Boltzmann factor is close to 1, so T is close to the identity\n\nBut this doesn't help with the actual proof.\n\nLet me look at what the literature says. The standard proof of reflection positivity for lattice gauge theories (Osterwalder-Seiler, Menz, etc.) has two parts:\n\n1. **Algebraic factorization**: Reduce ∫ f·θf to ∫ G·θG (done above)\n2. **Positivity of the integral**: Show ∫ G·θG ≥ 0 via transfer matrix argument\n\nThe transfer matrix argument for the Wilson action uses the fact that the Boltzmann factor exp(-β S_W) is reflection-positive, which is proven by expanding in the character expansion (high-temperature expansion) of the Wilson action.\n\nFor SU(N), the character expansion is:\nexp(β/N Re Tr(U)) = ∑_λ a_λ(β) χ_λ(U)\n\nwhere λ runs over irreducible representations of SU(N), χ_λ are characters, and a_λ(β) ≥ 0 for all λ (by a theorem of Osterwalder-Seiler, this is proven using the orthogonality relations of SU(N)).\n\nHmm, this is exactly the Peter-Weyl theorem territory.\n\nLet me think about a different approach: For a FINITE lattice, the transfer matrix acts on a FINITE-DIMENSIONAL space (if we discretize the group, or if we use a Fourier basis). But SU(N) is not finite...\n\nWait, but for a NUMERICAL simulation, one would discretize SU(N). But for a formal proof, we can't discretize.\n\nOK let me think about this more carefully. The space L²(SU(N)^(links)) is infinite-dimensional. But the transfer matrix has a special structure: its kernel is a product over independent plaquettes. Each plaquette involves at most 4 SU(N) variables. The positivity of the operator can be reduced to the positivity of a \"single-plaquette transfer matrix\" acting on L²(SU(N)⁴).\n\nFor a single plaquette crossing the interface, we can write the Boltzmann factor as:\nexp(β/N Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹))\n\nwhere g₁, g₂ are on one side of the interface and g₃, g₄ on the other.\n\nThe question is whether the function K(g₁, g₂, g₃, g₄) = exp(β/N Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹)) is a positive-definite kernel on SU(N)² × SU(N)².\n\nThis IS a known result: the function exp(β Re Tr(g)) on SU(N) is positive-definite (it's the heat kernel, which is the convolution of Gaussian measures on the Lie algebra). And the plaquette function is related to this by a change of variables.\n\nActually, this gives me an idea. Let me check if there's a simpler argument:\n\nThe function F(g) = exp(β/N Re Tr(g)) on SU(N) is positive-definite because it's the Fourier transform of a Gaussian measure on the Lie algebra su(N) (under the Kirillov correspondence, or more concretely, because it's the heat kernel at time β on SU(N)).\n\nThen for the plaquette, we can write the interface kernel as:\nK(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = ∏_{plaquettes crossing interface} F(plaquette product)\n\nAnd the product of positive-definite functions is positive-definite (this is a property of the tensor product of positive-definite kernels).\n\nThus the transfer matrix is positive.\n\nBut proving all of this in Lean... we need:\n1. The notion of positive-definite functions on a compact group\n2. The fact that the heat kernel on SU(N) is positive-definite\n3. The product of positive-definite functions is positive-definite\n4. The transfer matrix positivity follows\n\nThis is still substantial work, but it avoids the full Peter-Weyl theorem. The heat kernel approach might be simpler because it uses Gaussian integrals on the Lie algebra rather than representation theory.\n\nHowever, the heat kernel on SU(N) hasn't been formalized either. This is also a significant piece of work.\n\nLet me think about what's actually feasible in this session. Given that:\n- Lean/lake is not installed\n- I can't build or test the code\n- The gap is well-documented\n- The user asked me to do work\n\nMaybe the most productive thing is to:\n\n1. **Explore the existing literature** for a simpler proof of the transfer matrix positivity that might be easier to formalize\n2. **Search for existing Mathlib lemmas** that might be relevant\n3. **Consider starting Phase 7 work** that doesn't depend on the gap being resolved\n\nLet me search for some resources.\n\nActually, I want to check something. The user mentioned \"Phase 7: Continuum limit / OS reconstruct / mass gap\". What does Phase 7 entail exactly? Let me re-read the Overview.\n\nFrom Overview.lean:\n- Continuum limit (Balaban renormalization group)\n- OS reconstruction theorem (Euclidean → Minkowski reconstruction)\n- Mass gap proof (positivity and exponential decay)\n\nThese are long-term goals. The OS reconstruction theorem is independent of the specific lattice model — it's a theorem about any Euclidean QFT satisfying the OS axioms. So if we formalize the OS reconstruction theorem, it would apply to our lattice model once the reflection positivity gap is filled.\n\nActually, that's a good idea! The OS reconstruction theorem is a general theorem:\n1. Given a set of Euclidean Green's functions satisfying OS0-OS4\n2. There exists a Wightman QFT with the same time-ordered correlation functions\n\nThis is independent of whether the specific model (Yang-Mills on a lattice) satisfies the axioms. Formalizing the reconstruction theorem would be a significant piece of work, but it's pure functional analysis / representation theory of the Poincaré group — no gauge theory required.\n\nLet me search for more information about what's available in Mathlib for this.\n\nBut wait, I can't run Lean or check Mathlib. Let me search the web for the current state of Mathlib's representation theory.","turn":6,"is_prune_marker":false}
