{"id":34,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1783960337,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"paths\": [\"src/lean/YangMills/OSAxioms.lean\", \"src/lean/YangMills.lean\", \"src/lean/YangMills/SpecialUnitary.lean\"]}","name":"read_files"},"id":"call_00_H6loUEB7TZhSKOEZG4B32450","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":852,"reasoning_content":"The PDF is in scanned image format, so I can't read the text. Let me step back and think about what I should do.\n\nGiven the extensive exploration I've done, let me now formulate a plan. The situation is:\n\n1. Phase 6 is well-documented with one remaining `sorry`\n2. The remaining gap requires substantial mathematical infrastructure (Peter-Weyl theorem for SU(N))\n3. There may be simpler approaches (heat kernel, character expansion) but they all require significant work\n4. The project could benefit from parallel tracks: one working on the gap, another on Phase 7\n\nLet me now think about what concrete steps to take:\n\n**Option A: Start working on Phase 7 (OS reconstruction theorem)**\n\nThe Osterwalder-Schrader reconstruction theorem is a general theorem in axiomatic QFT that says: given a set of Euclidean Green's functions satisfying OS axioms (OS0-OS4), there exists a Wightman quantum field theory. This theorem is independent of the specific model and would apply to our lattice Yang-Mills measure once the reflection positivity gap is filled.\n\nThe OS reconstruction theorem involves:\n1. The Wightman axioms (Hilbert space, vacuum, fields, Poincaré covariance, spectral condition)\n2. The OS axioms (analyticity, symmetry, reflection positivity, cluster property)\n3. The reconstruction theorem: OS axioms → Wightman theory\n\nThis is a significant formalization project but is pure functional analysis / representation theory and doesn't require gauge theory specifics.\n\n**Option B: Start building the Peter-Weyl theorem infrastructure**\n\nThis would involve:\n1. The representation theory of compact Lie groups (irreps, characters)\n2. Peter-Weyl theorem: L²(G) ≅ ⊕ V_λ ⊗ V_λ^*\n3. Positive-definite functions on compact groups\n4. Bochner theorem for compact groups\n\nThis is also a large project and overlaps significantly with ongoing Mathlib work on representation theory.\n\n**Option C: Start on the continuum limit / Balaban RG**\n\nThis is about taking the lattice spacing to zero and showing convergence. It's very complex and relies on the lattice theory being well-defined (which it is, modulo the gap).\n\nI think the most productive approach is to **start working on the OS reconstruction theorem** (Option A). Here's why:\n1. It's independent of the remaining gap\n2. It's a well-understood mathematical theorem with a clear proof\n3. It connects the lattice theory to continuum QFT\n4. It doesn't require gauge theory specifics\n5. It would be a significant milestone even if the Yang-Mills-specific parts are incomplete\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read what the project overview says about Phase 7:\n\nFrom Overview.lean:\n> ### Future work:\n> - Complete the transfer matrix positivity proof (Peter–Weyl theorem)\n> - Construct the `PeriodicExpectation` structure (defined but uses `sorry`)\n> - Continuum limit (Balaban renormalization group)\n> - OS reconstruction theorem (Euclidean → Minkowski reconstruction)\n> - Mass gap proof (positivity and exponential decay)\n\nAnd from the project task list:\n> Phase 7: Continuum limit / OS reconstruct / mass gap (long-term)\n\nSo Phase 7 is about the continuum limit, OS reconstruction, and the mass gap. These are all massive undertakings.\n\nLet me think about what's most productive. The OS reconstruction theorem is well-defined and self-contained. Let me start by creating the basic structure for it.\n\nActually, looking at the existing code more carefully, there's already an `OSAxioms.lean` file. Let me read it.","turn":15,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":35,"role":"Tool","content":"path:C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\OSAxioms.lean\n-- 91 lines, 3072 bytes --\n 1 | /-\n 2 | # Osterwalder-Schrader Axioms (Euclidean QFT)\n 3 | \n 4 | Formalization of the Osterwalder-Schrader axioms for Euclidean quantum field\n 5 | theory. These are the Euclidean analog of the Wightman axioms and provide the\n 6 | rigorous foundation for constructive QFT via the OS reconstruction theorem.\n 7 | \n 8 | References:\n 9 | - K. Osterwalder, R. Schrader, \"Axioms for Euclidean Green's Functions\" (1973, 1975)\n10 | - J. Glimm, A. Jaffe, \"Quantum Physics: A Functional Integral Point of View\"\n11 | - R. Streater, A. Wightman, \"PCT, Spin and Statistics, and All That\"\n12 | -/\n13 | \n14 | import Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.ContDiff.Basic\n15 | \n16 | namespace YangMills\n17 | \n18 | namespace OS\n19 | \n20 | /--\n21 | Euclidean spacetime dimension. For Yang-Mills we work in d = 4.\n22 | -/\n23 | def spacetimeDimension : ℕ := 4\n24 | \n25 | /--\n26 | OS0: Temperedness — the Schwinger functions are tempered distributions on\n27 | ℝ^{dn} \\ {coincident points}.\n28 | \n29 | In a full formalization, a Schwinger function S_n is a tempered distribution\n30 | on (ℝ⁴)^n, symmetric under permutations of arguments, and defined on the\n31 | full space after removing coincident points.\n32 | -/\n33 | structure SchwingerFunction (n : ℕ) : Type 1 where\n34 |   /-- The underlying tempered distribution (place holder). -/\n35 |   distribution : Type\n36 | \n37 | /--\n38 | OS1: Euclidean covariance — Schwinger functions are invariant under the\n39 | Euclidean group E(4) = SO(4) ⋉ ℝ^4. For all g ∈ E(4):\n40 |   S_n(x_1, ..., x_n) = S_n(g·x_1, ..., g·x_n)\n41 | -/\n42 | structure EuclideanCovariance : Type 1 where\n43 |   /-- Invariance under simultaneous Euclidean transformations. -/\n44 |   invariant : ∀ (n : ℕ), SchwingerFunction n → SchwingerFunction n\n45 | \n46 | /--\n47 | OS2: Symmetry — Schwinger functions are symmetric under permutations of\n48 | their arguments. For any permutation π ∈ S_n:\n49 |   S_n(x_{π(1)}, ..., x_{π(n)}) = S_n(x_1, ..., x_n)\n50 | -/\n51 | structure Symmetry : Type 1 where\n52 |   symmetric : ∀ (n : ℕ), (SchwingerFunction n → SchwingerFunction n)\n53 | \n54 | /--\n55 | OS3: Reflection positivity (Osterwalder-Schrader positivity) — the key axiom.\n56 | \n57 | Let θ be the time-reflection map (x₀, x) ↦ (-x₀, x). Then for any finite\n58 | sequence of test functions f_i supported in t > 0:\n59 |   ∑_{i,j} S_{|i|+|j|}(f_i^* ⊗ (θ_* f_j)) ≥ 0\n60 | where |i| denotes the number of arguments of f_i.\n61 | -/\n62 | structure ReflectionPositive : Prop where\n63 |   /-- The reflection-positivity inequality (place holder). -/\n64 |   positive : ∀ (_ : Unit → Unit), True\n65 | \n66 | /--\n67 | OS4: Ergodicity (uniqueness of vacuum) — the vacuum vector is the unique\n68 | translation-invariant vector in the Hilbert space.\n69 | -/\n70 | structure Ergodicity : Prop where\n71 |   /-- Uniqueness of the translation-invariant vacuum state. -/\n72 |   uniqueVacuum : True\n73 | \n74 | /--\n75 | OS Reconstruction Theorem: Given a family of Schwinger functions satisfying\n76 | OS0-OS4, there exists a Wightman QFT (a Hilbert space, vacuum vector, and\n77 | field operators) whose Schwinger functions are the given ones.\n78 | \n79 | This is the Euclidean → Minkowski reconstruction.\n80 | -/\n81 | structure OSReconstruction : Type 1 where\n82 |   /-- The physical Hilbert space. -/\n83 |   hilbertSpace : Type\n84 |   /-- The vacuum vector. -/\n85 |   vacuumVector : Type\n86 |   /-- The Wightman field operators. -/\n87 |   fieldOperators : Type\n88 | \n89 | end OS\n90 | \n91 | end YangMills\n\n---\npath:C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills.lean\n-- 16 lines, 444 bytes --\n 1 | /-\n 2 | # Yang-Mills Formalization Project\n 3 | \n 4 | Lean 4 formalization of the Yang-Mills existence and mass gap problem.\n 5 | -/\n 6 | \n 7 | import YangMills.Overview\n 8 | import YangMills.OSAxioms\n 9 | import YangMills.GaugeTheory\n10 | import YangMills.Lattice\n11 | import YangMills.MassGap\n12 | import YangMills.Proofs.BasicLemmas\n13 | import YangMills.Proofs.GaugeInvariance\n14 | import YangMills.Proofs.JacobiIdentity\n15 | import YangMills.Proofs.LatticeMeasure\n16 | import YangMills.Proofs.ReflectionPositivity\n\n---\npath:C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\SpecialUnitary.lean\n-- 335 lines, 15653 bytes --\n  1 | /-\n  2 | # SU(N) Compact Lie Group\n  3 | \n  4 | Formalization of SU(N), the special unitary group, and its Lie algebra su(N)\n  5 | as the foundation for Yang-Mills gauge theory.\n  6 | \n  7 | We use Mathlib's existing matrix group implementations:\n  8 | - `Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup n ℂ` for SU(N)\n  9 | - `Matrix.unitaryGroup n ℂ` for U(N)\n 10 | - The Lie algebra su(N) is modelled as skew-Hermitian traceless matrices\n 11 | \n 12 | Reference: Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/UnitaryGroup.lean\n 13 | -/\n 14 | \n 15 | import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.UnitaryGroup\n 16 | import Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic\n 17 | import Mathlib.Algebra.Lie.Basic\n 18 | import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup\n 19 | import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace\n 20 | import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.ConjTranspose\n 21 | import Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Basic\n 22 | import Mathlib.Topology.Instances.Matrix\n 23 | import Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.Group.Basic\n 24 | import Mathlib.Topology.MetricSpace.Basic\n 25 | import Mathlib.Topology.MetricSpace.ProperSpace\n 26 | import Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Haar.Basic\n 27 | \n 28 | open Matrix\n 29 | open scoped Matrix\n 30 | open Set\n 31 | open Metric\n 32 | open Finset\n 33 | open MeasureTheory\n 34 | \n 35 | namespace YangMills\n 36 | \n 37 | /--\n 38 | The special unitary group SU(N) as unitary `N×N` complex matrices with determinant 1.\n 39 | Implemented as `Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ`.\n 40 | -/\n 41 | abbrev SU (N : ℕ) : Type :=\n 42 |   Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ\n 43 | \n 44 | -- ---------------------------------------------------------------\n 45 | -- Topological group structure\n 46 | -- ---------------------------------------------------------------\n 47 | \n 48 | lemma star_mem_specialUnitaryGroup (N : ℕ) : ∀ {x : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ},\n 49 |     x ∈ Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ → star x ∈ Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ := by\n 50 |   intro x hx\n 51 |   let x' : Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ := ⟨x, hx⟩\n 52 |   have hstar_prop : (star x').1 ∈ Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ := (star x').property\n 53 |   simpa [Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup.coe_star] using hstar_prop\n 54 | \n 55 | instance (N : ℕ) : ContinuousMul (SU N) :=\n 56 |   inferInstance\n 57 | \n 58 | instance (N : ℕ) : ContinuousStar (SU N) := by\n 59 |   have hstar_cont : Continuous (star : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) :=\n 60 |     ContinuousStar.continuous_star\n 61 |   have hstar_preserves : ∀ (x : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ),\n 62 |       x ∈ Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ → star x ∈ Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ :=\n 63 |     fun x hx => star_mem_specialUnitaryGroup N hx\n 64 |   have h_cont_map : Continuous (Subtype.map (star : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)\n 65 |       hstar_preserves) :=\n 66 |     hstar_cont.subtype_map hstar_preserves\n 67 |   have h_eq : (star : SU N → SU N) = \n 68 |       (Subtype.map (star : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) hstar_preserves) := by\n 69 |     ext x : 1\n 70 |     apply Subtype.ext\n 71 |     rfl\n 72 |   have h_cont_star : Continuous (star : SU N → SU N) := by\n 73 |     rw [h_eq]\n 74 |     exact h_cont_map\n 75 |   exact ⟨h_cont_star⟩\n 76 | \n 77 | instance (N : ℕ) : ContinuousInv (SU N) := by\n 78 |   have h_cont_star : Continuous (star : SU N → SU N) :=\n 79 |     ContinuousStar.continuous_star\n 80 |   have h_inv_eq_star : (fun (x : SU N) => x⁻¹) = (star : SU N → SU N) := by\n 81 |     ext x : 1\n 82 |     apply Subtype.ext\n 83 |     exact congrArg Subtype.val (Matrix.star_eq_inv x).symm\n 84 |   refine ⟨?continuous_inv⟩\n 85 |   rw [h_inv_eq_star]\n 86 |   exact h_cont_star\n 87 | \n 88 | instance (N : ℕ) : IsTopologicalGroup (SU N) where\n 89 | \n 90 | -- ---------------------------------------------------------------\n 91 | -- Borel structure (needed for Haar measure)\n 92 | -- ---------------------------------------------------------------\n 93 | \n 94 | noncomputable instance (N : ℕ) : MeasurableSpace (SU N) :=\n 95 |   borel _\n 96 | \n 97 | instance (N : ℕ) : BorelSpace (SU N) where\n 98 |   measurable_eq := rfl\n 99 | \n100 | -- ---------------------------------------------------------------\n101 | -- Compactness of SU(N)\n102 | -- ---------------------------------------------------------------\n103 | \n104 | lemma entry_norm_le_one (N : ℕ) (A : SU N) (i j : Fin N) : ‖A.1 i j‖ ≤ 1 := by\n105 |   rcases A.property with ⟨⟨huni1, huni2⟩, hdet⟩\n106 |   have h_diag : (star A.1 * A.1) j j = 1 := by\n107 |     rw [huni1]\n108 |     simp\n109 |   have h_sum_complex : (∑ k : Fin N, Complex.normSq (A.1 k j) : ℂ) = 1 := by\n110 |     calc\n111 |       (∑ k : Fin N, Complex.normSq (A.1 k j) : ℂ) = ∑ k : Fin N, ((starRingEnd ℂ) (A.1 k j) * A.1 k j) := by\n112 |         simp [Complex.normSq_eq_conj_mul_self]\n113 |       _ = (star A.1 * A.1) j j := by\n114 |         simp [Matrix.mul_apply, Matrix.star_apply]\n115 |       _ = 1 := h_diag\n116 |   have h_sum_real : ∑ k : Fin N, Complex.normSq (A.1 k j) = 1 := by\n117 |     exact_mod_cast h_sum_complex\n118 |   have h_nonneg : ∀ k : Fin N, 0 ≤ Complex.normSq (A.1 k j) :=\n119 |     λ k => Complex.normSq_nonneg _\n120 |   have h_bound_sq : Complex.normSq (A.1 i j) ≤ 1 := by\n121 |     have : Complex.normSq (A.1 i j) ≤ ∑ k : Fin N, Complex.normSq (A.1 k j) :=\n122 |       Finset.single_le_sum (λ k hk => h_nonneg k) (Finset.mem_univ i)\n123 |     linarith\n124 |   calc\n125 |     ‖A.1 i j‖ = Real.sqrt (Complex.normSq (A.1 i j)) := by rw [Complex.norm_def]\n126 |     _ ≤ Real.sqrt 1 := Real.sqrt_le_sqrt h_bound_sq\n127 |     _ = 1 := by norm_num\n128 | lemma trace_re_bound (N : ℕ) (A : SU N) : |(trace ((A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ))).re| ≤ N := by\n129 |   have h_entry : ∀ i : Fin N, |(A.1 i i).re| ≤ 1 := by\n130 |     intro i\n131 |     have h_norm : ‖A.1 i i‖ ≤ 1 := entry_norm_le_one N A i i\n132 |     have h_re_norm : |(A.1 i i).re| ≤ ‖A.1 i i‖ := Complex.abs_re_le_norm _\n133 |     linarith\n134 |   have h_trace_re : (trace ((A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ))).re = ∑ i : Fin N, (A.1 i i).re := by\n135 |     simp [Matrix.trace, map_sum]\n136 |   rw [h_trace_re]\n137 |   calc\n138 |     |∑ i : Fin N, (A.1 i i).re| ≤ ∑ i : Fin N, |(A.1 i i).re| := abs_sum_le_sum_abs _ _\n139 |     _ ≤ ∑ i : Fin N, (1 : ℝ) := Finset.sum_le_sum (λ i hi => h_entry i)\n140 |     _ = (N : ℝ) := by simp\n141 | \n142 | -- ---------------------------------------------------------------\n143 | -- Compactness of SU(N)\n144 | -- ---------------------------------------------------------------\n145 | \n146 | lemma continuous_det (N : ℕ) : Continuous (Matrix.det : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ → ℂ) := by\n147 |   have h_eq : (Matrix.det : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ → ℂ) = \n148 |     λ A => ∑ σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin N), Equiv.Perm.sign σ • ∏ i : Fin N, A (σ i) i := by\n149 |     ext A; exact Matrix.det_apply A\n150 |   rw [h_eq]\n151 |   refine continuous_finsetSum _ (λ σ hσ => ?_)\n152 |   have h_prod : Continuous (λ (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) => ∏ i : Fin N, A (σ i) i) := by\n153 |     refine continuous_finsetProd _ (λ i hi => ?_)\n154 |     have h1 : Continuous (λ (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) => A (σ i)) :=\n155 |       continuous_apply (σ i)\n156 |     have h2 : Continuous (λ (f : (Fin N) → ℂ) => f i) :=\n157 |       continuous_apply i\n158 |     exact h2.comp h1\n159 |   exact (continuous_const.smul h_prod)\n160 | \n161 | lemma isClosed_SU_range (N : ℕ) : IsClosed (Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) := by\n162 |   let f1 : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ := λ A => A * star A\n163 |   let f2 : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ → ℂ := Matrix.det\n164 |   have h_cont_f1 : Continuous f1 :=\n165 |     Continuous.mul continuous_id ContinuousStar.continuous_star\n166 |   have h_cont_f2 : Continuous f2 := continuous_det N\n167 |   have h_closed_1 : IsClosed ({1} : Set (Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) := isClosed_singleton\n168 |   have h_closed_2 : IsClosed ({1} : Set ℂ) := isClosed_singleton\n169 |   have h_closed_inter : IsClosed (f1⁻¹' ({1} : Set (Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) ∩ f2⁻¹' ({1} : Set ℂ)) :=\n170 |     IsClosed.inter (IsClosed.preimage h_cont_f1 h_closed_1) (IsClosed.preimage h_cont_f2 h_closed_2)\n171 |   have h_eq : Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) = \n172 |     f1⁻¹' ({1} : Set (Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) ∩ f2⁻¹' ({1} : Set ℂ) := by\n173 |     ext A\n174 |     constructor\n175 |     · rintro ⟨A', rfl⟩\n176 |       rcases A'.property with ⟨hA'_unitary, hA'_det⟩\n177 |       have hA' : (A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) * star (A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) = 1 := by\n178 |         simpa using Matrix.mem_unitaryGroup_iff.mp hA'_unitary\n179 |       have hdet : Matrix.det (A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) = 1 := by\n180 |         simpa [MonoidHom.mem_mker] using hA'_det\n181 |       simp [f1, f2, hA', hdet]\n182 |     · rintro ⟨hA_star, hA_det⟩\n183 |       have hA_unitary : A ∈ Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ := by\n184 |         rw [Matrix.mem_unitaryGroup_iff]\n185 |         exact hA_star\n186 |       have hA_su : A ∈ Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ := by\n187 |         rw [Matrix.mem_specialUnitaryGroup_iff]\n188 |         exact ⟨hA_unitary, hA_det⟩\n189 |       exact ⟨⟨A, hA_su⟩, rfl⟩\n190 |   rw [h_eq]\n191 |   exact h_closed_inter\n192 | \n193 | lemma norm_pi_le_of_forall (N : ℕ) (f : (Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ) (hf : ∀ i j, ‖f i j‖ ≤ 1) : ‖f‖ ≤ 1 := by\n194 |   rw [Pi.norm_def]\n195 |   have h_row : ∀ (i : Fin N), ‖f i‖ ≤ 1 := by\n196 |     intro i\n197 |     rw [Pi.norm_def]\n198 |     have h_sup_row : Finset.univ.sup (fun (j : Fin N) => ‖f i j‖₊) ≤ (1 : NNReal) := by\n199 |       apply Finset.sup_le\n200 |       intro j hj\n201 |       have h_entry : ‖f i j‖ ≤ 1 := hf i j\n202 |       exact_mod_cast h_entry\n203 |     exact_mod_cast h_sup_row\n204 |   have h_sup : Finset.univ.sup (fun (i : Fin N) => ‖f i‖₊) ≤ (1 : NNReal) := by\n205 |     apply Finset.sup_le\n206 |     intro i hi\n207 |     have h_row' : ‖f i‖ ≤ 1 := h_row i\n208 |     exact_mod_cast h_row'\n209 |   exact_mod_cast h_sup\n210 | \n211 | /-- The homeomorphism between matrices and Pi types. -/\n212 | def matrixPiHomeomorph (N : ℕ) : Homeomorph (Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) ((Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ) := by\n213 |   refine {\n214 |     toFun := λ A i j => A i j\n215 |     invFun := λ f => λ i j => f i j\n216 |     left_inv := λ A => rfl\n217 |     right_inv := λ f => rfl\n218 |     continuous_toFun := by continuity\n219 |     continuous_invFun := by continuity\n220 |   }\n221 | \n222 | /-- Homeomorphism between SU N and its image in matrices. -/\n223 | noncomputable def SUHomeomorphRange (N : ℕ) : Homeomorph (SU N) (Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) := by\n224 |   have h_iff : ∀ (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ), \n225 |       A ∈ Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup (Fin N) ℂ ↔ A ∈ Set.range ((fun (A' : SU N) => (A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) := by\n226 |     intro A\n227 |     constructor\n228 |     · intro hA\n229 |       exact ⟨⟨A, hA⟩, rfl⟩\n230 |     · rintro ⟨A', rfl⟩\n231 |       exact A'.property\n232 |   apply Homeomorph.subtype (Homeomorph.refl (Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) h_iff\n233 | \n234 | noncomputable instance (N : ℕ) : CompactSpace (SU N) := by\n235 |   -- Strategy: SU(N) is homeomorphic to its image in matrices, which is compact\n236 | \n237 |   -- Step 1: The closed unit ball in the Pi type is compact\n238 |   have h_ball_compact : IsCompact (closedBall (0 : (Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ) 1) :=\n239 |     isCompact_closedBall (0 : (Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ) 1\n240 | \n241 |   -- Step 2: Its image under the homeomorphism inverse is compact in matrices\n242 |   have h_compact_mat : IsCompact ((matrixPiHomeomorph N).symm '' closedBall (0 : (Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ) 1) :=\n243 |     (matrixPiHomeomorph N).symm.isCompact_image.mpr h_ball_compact\n244 | \n245 |   -- Step 3: The range of SU N in matrices is a subset of this compact set\n246 |   have h_range_subset : Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) ⊆\n247 |       (matrixPiHomeomorph N).symm '' closedBall (0 : (Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ) 1 := by\n248 |     intro A hA\n249 |     rcases hA with ⟨A', rfl⟩\n250 |     have h_entry : ∀ i j, ‖(A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) i j‖ ≤ 1 :=\n251 |       entry_norm_le_one N A'\n252 |     have h_norm : ‖(λ i j => (A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) i j)‖ ≤ 1 :=\n253 |       norm_pi_le_of_forall N (λ i j => (A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) i j) h_entry\n254 |     have h_mem : (λ i j => (A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) i j) ∈ closedBall (0 : (Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ) 1 := by\n255 |       rw [Metric.mem_closedBall, dist_eq_norm, sub_zero]; exact h_norm\n256 |     refine ⟨(λ i j => (A' : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) i j), h_mem, ?_⟩\n257 |     simp [matrixPiHomeomorph]\n258 | \n259 |   -- Step 4: The range is closed\n260 |   have h_range_closed : IsClosed (Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) :=\n261 |     isClosed_SU_range N\n262 | \n263 |   -- Step 5: The range is compact (closed subset of a compact set)\n264 |   have h_range_compact : IsCompact (Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) := by\n265 |     have h_inter_eq : ((matrixPiHomeomorph N).symm '' closedBall (0 : (Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ) 1) ∩\n266 |       Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) =\n267 |       Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) :=\n268 |       Set.inter_eq_right.mpr h_range_subset\n269 |     rw [← h_inter_eq]\n270 |     exact IsCompact.inter_right h_compact_mat h_range_closed\n271 | \n272 |   -- Step 6: Use isCompact_iff_compactSpace and the homeomorphism\n273 |   have h_compact_range_type : CompactSpace (Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) :=\n274 |     (isCompact_iff_compactSpace.mp h_range_compact)\n275 | \n276 |   -- SU N is homeomorphic to this range, so CompactSpace transfers\n277 |   exact Homeomorph.compactSpace (SUHomeomorphRange N).symm\n278 | \n279 | /-- SU(N) is second-countable because it embeds into the Euclidean space of matrices. -/\n280 | instance (N : ℕ) : SecondCountableTopology (SU N) := by\n281 |   -- The image of SU(N) under the embedding into matrices is compact, hence second-countable.\n282 |   -- Since `SU N` is homeomorphic to its image, it is also second-countable.\n283 |   haveI h_image : SecondCountableTopology (Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) := by\n284 |     -- The range is a subset of `Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ` which is second-countable\n285 |     -- because it is homeomorphic to `(Fin N) → (Fin N) → ℂ` (finite product of ℂ).\n286 |     haveI : SecondCountableTopology (Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ) :=\n287 |       (matrixPiHomeomorph N).secondCountableTopology\n288 |     -- The image is a subspace, hence second-countable (via `Subtype.secondCountableTopology` instance)\n289 |     infer_instance\n290 |   -- SU(N) is homeomorphic to its image, hence second-countable\n291 |   have h_homeo : Homeomorph (SU N) (Set.range ((fun (A : SU N) => (A : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) : SU N → Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ)) :=\n292 |     SUHomeomorphRange N\n293 |   exact h_homeo.secondCountableTopology\n294 | /--\n295 | The dimension of SU(N) as a real Lie group is N² - 1.\n296 | -/\n297 | def dimensionSU (N : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n298 |   N*N - 1\n299 | \n300 | /--\n301 | The Lie algebra su(N): skew-Hermitian traceless complex matrices.\n302 | As a real vector space, these are `X ∈ M_N(ℂ)` such that `X† = -X` and `Tr(X) = 0`.\n303 | -/\n304 | structure LieAlgebraSU (N : ℕ) : Type where\n305 |   /-- The underlying matrix. -/\n306 |   matrix : Matrix (Fin N) (Fin N) ℂ\n307 |   /-- Skew-Hermitian condition: X† = -X. -/\n308 |   skewHermitian : matrixᴴ = -matrix\n309 |   /-- Traceless condition: Tr(X) = 0. -/\n310 |   traceless : trace matrix = 0\n311 | \n312 | /--\n313 | The structure constants of su(N) in a basis {T^a}: [T^a, T^b] = i f^{abc} T^c\n314 | Here `f : Fin dim → Fin dim → Fin dim → ℝ` are the totally antisymmetric structure constants.\n315 | -/\n316 | structure StructureConstants (N : ℕ) : Type 1 where\n317 |   /-- The dimension of the Lie algebra. -/\n318 |   dim : ℕ\n319 |   /-- The totally antisymmetric structure constants f^{abc} -/\n320 |   f : (Fin dim) → (Fin dim) → (Fin dim) → ℝ\n321 |   /-- Antisymmetry in first two indices -/\n322 |   antisymm : ∀ a b c, f a b c = - f b a c\n323 |   /-- Jacobi identity -/\n324 |   jacobi : ∀ a b c d e : Fin dim,\n325 |     f a b c * f c d e + f a d c * f c b e + f a e c * f c d b = 0\n326 | \n327 | /--\n328 | The matrix exponential map exp : su(N) → SU(N).\n329 | In a full formalization this would use a matrix exponential function.\n330 | Mathlib does not currently have a general matrix exponential, so this is a placeholder.\n331 | -/\n332 | noncomputable def exponentialMap (N : ℕ) (_X : LieAlgebraSU N) : SU N :=\n333 |   1\n334 | \n335 | end YangMills\n\n---\n","timestamp":1783960338,"token_count":6654,"tool_call_id":"call_00_H6loUEB7TZhSKOEZG4B32450","full_token_estimate":4857,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_files","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/OSAxioms.lean,