{"id":39,"role":"Tool","content":"---\ndescription: Knowledge base for the Yang–Mills mass gap Clay Millennium problem. Includes proven results, active programs, failure modes of claimed solutions, and plain-language references across physics, lattice, and constructive QFT literature.\n---\n\n# Yang–Mills Mass Gap\n\nA working knowledge base for the Clay Millennium Prize problem. See `yangmills.txt` at project root for the full living document.\n\n## The Problem\n\nConstruct a non-trivial 4D quantum Yang–Mills theory (any compact simple gauge group G) satisfying Wightman or Osterwalder–Schrader axioms, and prove a mass gap Δ > 0 (strictly positive minimum energy above vacuum).\n\n## Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Wightman axioms | 1964 formal definition of a legitimate QFT |\n| OS axioms | Euclidean-time equivalent; includes reflection positivity |\n| Mass gap (Δ) | Energy gap between vacuum and lowest excited state |\n| Lattice gauge theory | Discretized spacetime, computer-simulable |\n| Continuum limit | Lattice spacing → 0 (the unsolved part) |\n| Asymptotic freedom | Coupling weakens at short distance (proven 1973) |\n| Confinement | Quarks/gluons never isolated (physics-rigor, not constructive) |\n| Wilson loop / string tension | Order parameter for confinement; area-law ⇒ confining |\n| Transfer operator | Time evolution on lattice; spectral gap in it ≈ lattice Δ |\n| Regularity structures | Hairer's machinery for ill-posed stochastic PDEs |\n| Reflection positivity | Key OS condition; most rigor attempts hinge on it |\n\n## Established Results\n\n- **2D Yang–Mills**: fully rigorous construction (Lévy 2003; Chevyrev 2019)\n- **3D Yang–Mills–Higgs**: rigorous stochastic construction with Higgs field (CCHS 2024)\n- **3D state space for YM**: rigorous candidate defined (Cao–Chatterjee 2024)\n- **Large-N lattice YM (strong coupling)**: rigorously solved (Chatterjee 2019)\n- **Probabilistic confinement**: rigorous mechanism in restricted setting (Chatterjee 2021)\n- **Mass gap on lattice**: numerically confirmed, not a continuum proof\n- **Lightest glueball** (0⁺⁺ pure SU(3)): m ≈ 1.73 ± 0.07 GeV (Morningstar & Peardon 1999)\n\n## Active Programs\n\n| Program | Lead(s) | Frontier |\n|---|---|---|\n| Regularity structures / stochastic quantization | Hairer, Chevyrev, Chandra, Shen | Push to pure 3D, then 4D |\n| Probabilistic/lattice methods | Chatterjee, Cao | Continuum limit via random surfaces |\n| Geometric analysis (YM heat flow) | L. Gross | Extending heat-flow to 4D |\n| Classical constructive QFT | Balaban; Federbush; Magnen–Rivasseau–Sénéor | Largely stalled |\n\n## Failure Modes of Claimed Solutions\n\n- **Pattern A**: redefine terms outside Wightman/OS, declare victory\n- **Pattern B**: physics-style argument in math notation, skips 4D renormalization\n- Recent claims (2024–26): none accepted by constructive-QFT community; most fail Pattern A or B\n\n## Tools Available\n\n- `verify_proof` — full proof checker: auto-detects verifier system (Lean/Coq/Z3), discovers a backend via `$AUTOCODE_VERIFIER` / `verify/<system>.sh` / PATH, runs the verifier as a subprocess with timeout, parses output for success/failure markers, runs Yang-Mills mass-gap structural sanity checks (Pattern A/B failure modes + required-ingredient checklist), and logs every attempt to `proofs/attempts.jsonl` (JSONL) so the agent can read its prior attempts with read_file/grep and avoid repeating dead ends. Implementation: `crates/ai/src/chat/tools/proof.rs`.\n- `search_literature` — search arXiv and web for papers by keyword/topic\n- `explore_theorem` — decompose a theorem into sub-goals and track exploration state\n\n## Usage\n\nLoad this skill when the user's task involves Yang–Mills theory, quantum field theory rigor, or the Clay Millennium problems. The living knowledge base `yangmills.txt` at project root contains the full citation-dense document with timeline, claimed-solution catalog, and open-question log.\n","timestamp":1783960349,"token_count":1218,"tool_call_id":"call_00_Hlno9C9wyiIKrdF4Y9RM6293","full_token_estimate":898,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"get_skill","file_path":"yang_mills_mass_gap","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":3934,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":1,"edit_line":null},"turn":17,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":40,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1783960367,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\": \"**/yangmills.txt\"}","name":"glob"},"id":"call_00_Hogl7swhmlXWIMuZOWmM6373","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":1524,"reasoning_content":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding. Let me plan what to do next.\n\nKey insight: The remaining gap in Phase 6 requires the Peter-Weyl theorem on SU(N), which is a significant piece of representation theory. However, there's been some work in Mathlib on representation theory of compact Lie groups, and the Haar measure on SU(N) is already available.\n\nI think the best approach for this session is to:\n1. Document the gap analysis in more detail\n2. Start preparatory work on the OS reconstruction theorem (Phase 7)\n3. Create a detailed plan for the Peter-Weyl theorem path\n\nBut actually, let me reconsider. The user's briefing says the next session should focus on one of three options for the gap. Rather than starting Phase 7 work (which would be a huge undertaking), maybe I should focus on doing research into what Mathlib already has, and potentially creating the Lean code structure for the Peter-Weyl theorem on SU(N).\n\nBut I can't run Lean or inspect Mathlib, so I can't check what's available.\n\nLet me focus on what I CAN do:\n1. Create a detailed proof sketch for the remaining gap with specific references\n2. Create a plan for the Peter-Weyl theorem formalization\n3. Potentially start writing the Lean code for the Peter-Weyl theorem (even though it can't be tested)\n\nActually, let me think about whether there's a SIMPLER approach to the remaining gap that avoids the full Peter-Weyl theorem.\n\nThe key inequality is:\n∫ G(U) G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n\nwhere G(U) = f(U) exp(-β S_OS⁺(U)) exp(-β S_OS_int(U)/2).\n\nSince the measure μ₀ factorizes as μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰ (positive, negative, interface links), we have:\n\n∫ G(U) G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫_{U⁺,U⁰} ∫_{U⁻} G(U⁺,U⁰) G(U⁻,U⁰) exp(-β S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nNow, define the function ψ(U⁺,U⁰) = G(U⁺,U⁰), and the integral operator T:\n\n(Tψ)(U⁺,U⁰) = ∫ ψ(U⁻,U⁰) K(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nwhere K(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰) = exp(-β S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)).\n\nThen ∫ G·θG dμ₀ = ∫ ψ(U⁺,U⁰) (Tψ)(U⁺,U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰) = ⟨ψ, Tψ⟩.\n\nSo the question is whether T is a positive operator on L²(μ⁺⊗μ⁰).\n\nFor the Wilson action, the interface action S_OS_int is a sum over plaquettes that cross the time-0 interface. Each such plaquette involves exactly two links on the positive side and two on the negative side (or similar). The key is that the integral kernel K factorizes as a product over these interface plaquettes.\n\nNow, here's a potentially simpler approach: Instead of using the Peter-Weyl theorem, we can use the HOS (Higher Order Singular) approach or the \"positivity of the heat kernel\" approach.\n\nFor the Wilson action, the Boltzmann weight is:\nexp(-β S_OS_int) = ∏_{interface plaquettes p} exp(β/N Re Tr(U_∂p))\n\nEach plaquette term involves exactly 4 link variables. For a plaquette crossing the interface, say with links U₁, U₂ on the positive side and U₃⁻¹, U₄⁻¹ on the negative side (with appropriate ordering), we have:\n\nexp(β/N Re Tr(U₁ U₂ U₃⁻¹ U₄⁻¹))\n\nThe key observation is that this function on SU(N)⁴ is positive-definite as a function of (U₁, U₂) for fixed (U₃, U₄), and vice versa.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. There's a well-known result: the function f(g) = exp(c Re Tr(g)) on SU(N) is positive-definite for c > 0. This is because it's the heat kernel on SU(N) at time c (up to normalization). \n\nThe heat kernel on SU(N) is positive-definite because:\n1. It's the convolution of the heat kernel at smaller times\n2. The heat kernel can be expressed as e^{tΔ}δ_e (the fundamental solution of the heat equation on the group)\n3. The operator e^{tΔ} is positive on L²(SU(N))\n\nNow, the plaquette term exp(β/N Re Tr(g₁ g₂ g₃⁻¹ g₄⁻¹)) is related to the heat kernel by a change of variables. Specifically, if we fix g₁, g₂, g₄ and vary g₃⁻¹, the plaquette function is the heat kernel evaluated at g₃ = g₁ g₂ (up to an inverse).\n\nActually, this is getting quite complicated. Let me take a step back and think about what's most productive.\n\nI think the best approach is:\n1. **Document the exact gap** — I've already done extensive documentation\n2. **Create a roadmap** for closing the gap via either the Peter-Weyl theorem or a simpler heat kernel approach\n3. **Start on Phase 7 preparatory work** — the OS reconstruction theorem\n\nLet me focus on creating concrete Lean code that makes progress. I'll start with the OS reconstruction theorem infrastructure, which is independent of the gap.\n\nActually, looking at the existing OSAxioms.lean, it's very sparse — just stubs. Let me think about what would be the most useful contribution.\n\nThe OS reconstruction theorem states: Given a family of Schwinger functions {S_n} satisfying OS0-OS4, there exists a Wightman QFT.\n\nThe main steps are:\n1. **Hilbert space construction**: Define the space of test functions, quotient by the null space of the reflection-positive inner product, and complete.\n2. **Field operators**: Construct the time-0 field operators from the Schwinger functions.\n3. **Poincaré covariance**: Extend Euclidean covariance to Minkowski covariance via analytic continuation.\n\nThis is a massive formalization project. But we can start with the Hilbert space construction, which is the core of the reflection positivity argument.\n\nActually, let me think about priorities. The user's project has a well-defined Phase 6 remaining gap. The documentation is thorough. What would be MOST useful for the next session?\n\nI think the most useful thing I can do is:\n1. Ensure the documentation is complete and accurate\n2. Create a detailed plan for the Peter-Weyl theorem approach\n3. Maybe start writing some of the representation theory infrastructure\n\nBut actually, there's something else I should check. Let me look