{"id":43,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\ReflectionPositivity.lean\n-- 1511 lines, 93799 bytes --\n1300 |     dsimp [f, g]\n1301 |     by_cases hi : (¬ plaquettePositive T L p ∧ ¬ plaquetteNegative T L p)\n1302 |     · rw [if_pos hi, if_pos ((h_interface_inv p).mpr hi)]\n1303 |       rw [← plaquetteContribution_reflect_eq_all N T L β U p]\n1304 |     · rw [if_neg hi, if_neg (mt (h_interface_inv p).mp hi)]\n1305 |   calc\n1306 |     ∑ p : PlaquetteIndex T L, f p = ∑ p : PlaquetteIndex T L, f (e p) := by\n1307 |       symm\n1308 |       exact Fintype.sum_equiv e (λ p => f (e p)) f (λ p => rfl)\n1309 |     _ = ∑ p : PlaquetteIndex T L, g p := by\n1310 |       refine Finset.sum_congr rfl (λ p hp => ?_)\n1311 |       rw [h_eq p]\n1312 | lemma gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n1313 |     (hT : Odd T) (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ) :\n1314 |     0 ≤ gibbsExpectation N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n1315 |       (λ U => f U * reflectObservable N f U) := by\n1316 |   dsimp [gibbsExpectation]\n1317 |   have hZ_pos : partitionFunctionFinite N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) > 0 :=\n1318 |     partitionFunctionFinite_pos N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n1319 |   refine div_nonneg ?_ (le_of_lt hZ_pos)\n1320 |   set μ₀ := productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) with hμ₀\n1321 |   set S_total := wilsonActionFiniteConfig N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) with hS\n1322 |   set U_ext := extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) with hU\n1323 |   have h_total_decomp : ∀ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1324 |       S_total cfg = wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β (U_ext cfg) +\n1325 |         wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β (U_ext cfg) +\n1326 |         wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) := by\n1327 |     intro cfg\n1328 |     calc\n1329 |       S_total cfg = wilsonActionFinite N β (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) (U_ext cfg) := rfl\n1330 |       _ = wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β (U_ext cfg) +\n1331 |           wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β (U_ext cfg) +\n1332 |           wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) :=\n1333 |         total_decomposition_os_periodic N T L β (U_ext cfg)\n1334 |   have h_neg_reflect : ∀ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1335 |       wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β (U_ext cfg) =\n1336 |       wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) := by\n1337 |     intro cfg\n1338 |     exact neg_action_reflection_os_periodic N T L β hT (U_ext cfg)\n1339 |   have h_int_reflect : ∀ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1340 |       wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) =\n1341 |       wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) := by\n1342 |     intro cfg\n1343 |     exact interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic N T L β hT (U_ext cfg)\n1344 |   have h_factorization : ∀ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1345 |       Real.exp (-β * S_total cfg) = \n1346 |       (Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β (U_ext cfg)) *\n1347 |         Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) / 2)) *\n1348 |       (Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg))) *\n1349 |         Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) / 2)) := by\n1350 |     intro cfg\n1351 |     rw [h_total_decomp cfg]\n1352 |     have h_mul : -β * (wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β (U_ext cfg) + wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β (U_ext cfg) +\n1353 |       wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg)) = \n1354 |       (-β * wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β (U_ext cfg)) + \n1355 |       (-β * wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β (U_ext cfg)) + \n1356 |       (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg)) := by ring\n1357 |     rw [h_mul]\n1358 |     rw [Real.exp_add, Real.exp_add]\n1359 |     rw [h_neg_reflect cfg, h_int_reflect cfg]\n1360 |     have h_exp_split : Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg)) =\n1361 |       Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) / 2) *\n1362 |       Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) / 2) := by\n1363 |       calc\n1364 |         Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg)) =\n1365 |           Real.exp ((-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) / 2) +\n1366 |                    (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) / 2)) := by ring\n1367 |         _ = Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) / 2) *\n1368 |             Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (U_ext cfg) / 2) := by rw [Real.exp_add]\n1369 |     rw [h_exp_split]\n1370 |     ring\n1371 |   set G := λ (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) =>\n1372 |     f U * Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β U) *\n1373 |     Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U / 2) with hG\n1374 |   have h_integrand_eq : ∀ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1375 |       f (U_ext cfg) * f (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) *\n1376 |       Real.exp (-β * S_total cfg) =\n1377 |       G (U_ext cfg) * G (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) := by\n1378 |     intro cfg\n1379 |     dsimp [G]\n1380 |     rw [h_factorization cfg]\n1381 |     ring\n1382 |   have h_fun_eq : (λ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =>\n1383 |       f (U_ext cfg) * f (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) * Real.exp (-β * S_total cfg)) =\n1384 |     (λ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =>\n1385 |       G (U_ext cfg) * G (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg))) := by\n1386 |     ext cfg; exact h_integrand_eq cfg\n1387 |   calc\n1388 |     (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1389 |       f (U_ext cfg) * f (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) * Real.exp (-β * S_total cfg) ∂ μ₀)\n1390 |         = (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1391 |       G (U_ext cfg) * G (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) ∂ μ₀) := by\n1392 |       rw [h_fun_eq]\n1393 |     _ ≥ 0 := by\n1394 |       -- GOAL: Prove that ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0.\n1395 |       --\n1396 |       -- This is the core of the Osterwalder–Seiler reflection positivity proof.\n1397 |       -- The integrand G·θG is NOT pointwise non-negative; the non-negativity\n1398 |       -- holds only after integration with respect to the product Haar measure.\n1399 |       --\n1400 |       -- MATHEMATICAL ARGUMENT (Osterwalder–Seiler 1979, §3):\n1401 |       --\n1402 |       -- The product Haar measure μ₀ on the finite periodic lattice factorises\n1403 |       -- as μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰, where μ⁺/μ⁻/μ⁰ are the Haar measures on links\n1404 |       -- in the positive-time, negative-time, and interface regions respectively.\n1405 |       --\n1406 |       -- Define the transfer matrix T acting on L²(μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁰) by:\n1407 |       --\n1408 |       --   (Tψ)(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ ψ(U⁻, U⁰) · exp(-β · S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n1409 |       --\n1410 |       -- where S_OS_int is the interface part of the Wilson action.  The key\n1411 |       -- identity (proved algebraically above) is:\n1412 |       --\n1413 |       --   ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ⟨G⁺, T·G⁺⟩_{L²(μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁰)}\n1414 |       --\n1415 |       -- where G⁺ is the restriction of G to variables in the positive-time and\n1416 |       -- interface regions.  The operator T is POSITIVE because the integral kernel\n1417 |       -- K(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = exp(-β · S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) is a positive-definite\n1418 |       -- function on (SU(N)ⁿ × SU(N)ⁿ).  This follows from the Peter–Weyl theorem\n1419 |       -- on SU(N): the matrix elements of the unitary irreducible representations\n1420 |       -- form an orthonormal basis of L²(SU(N)), and the Boltzmann factor expands\n1421 |       -- as a sum of squares in this basis (OS 1979, Theorem 3.1).\n1422 |       --\n1423 |       -- Consequently, ⟨G⁺, T·G⁺⟩ ≥ 0, and the integral is non-negative.\n1424 |       --\n1425 |       -- FORMALIZATION STATUS:\n1426 |       --\n1427 |       -- The Peter–Weyl theorem for SU(N) (or more generally for compact Lie groups)\n1428 |       -- is not yet available in Mathlib.  The spectral theory of compact integral\n1429 |       -- operators on L² is also not yet formalised.  Filling this gap requires:\n1430 |       --\n1431 |       --   1. The Peter–Weyl theorem: L²(SU(N)) ≅ ⊕_{λ} V_λ ⊗ V_λ^* (as\n1432 |       --      SU(N)×SU(N)-representations), where λ runs over dominant weights.\n1433 |       --   2. The Bochner–Minlos theorem or its Lie-group analogue: positive-definite\n1434 |       --      functions correspond to measures / states on the group C*-algebra.\n1435 |       --   3. Positivity of the transfer matrix T constructed from the Wilson\n1436 |       --      interface action.\n1437 |       --\n1438 |       -- REFERENCES:\n1439 |       --   - K. Osterwalder, E. Seiler, \"Gauge Field Theories on a Lattice\"\n1440 |       --     (Ann. Phys. 110, 1978, pp 440–471), §3.\n1441 |       --   - J. Glimm, A. Jaffe, \"Quantum Physics\" (2nd ed.), §6.1.\n1442 |       --   - M. Salmhofer, \"Construction of a Higgs field\" (thesis, 1990).\n1443 |       --\n1444 |       -- Until these results are available in Mathlib, the last step remains\n1445 |       -- an open `sorry`.  The rest of the reflection positivity proof (all\n1446 |       -- algebraic identities, the action decomposition, and the reduction\n1447 |       -- to the integral of G·θG) is fully formalised.\n1448 |       sorry\n1449 | end OSDecomposition\n1450 | /--\n1451 | The `PeriodicExpectation` structure for the SU(N) Wilson action on a finite periodic lattice.\n1452 | This provides a reflection positivity certificate that does NOT require the `hadd` hypothesis,\n1453 | because periodic boundary conditions make the lattice closed under forward time translation.\n1454 | -/\n1455 | structure PeriodicExpectation (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T) : Type 1 where\n1456 |   /-- The partition function Z = ∫ exp(-S_W) dU. -/\n1457 |   partitionFunction : ℝ\n1458 |   /-- Positivity of the partition function: Z > 0 for β > 0. -/\n1459 |   partitionFunctionPos : partitionFunction > 0\n1460 |   /-- The expectation of an observable F. -/\n1461 |   evaluate : (LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ) → ℝ\n1462 |   /-- Linearity of expectation. -/\n1463 |   linearity : ∀ (a b : ℝ) (F G : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ),\n1464 |     (MeasureTheory.Integrable (λ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =>\n1465 |       F (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg) *\n1466 |       Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfig N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg))\n1467 |       (productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)))) →\n1468 |     (MeasureTheory.Integrable (λ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =>\n1469 |       G (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg) *\n1470 |       Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfig N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg))\n1471 |       (productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)))) →\n1472 |     evaluate (λ U => a * F U + b * G U) = a * evaluate F + b * evaluate G\n1473 |   /-- Normalization: ⟨1⟩ = 1. -/\n1474 |   normalization : evaluate (λ _ => 1) = 1\n1475 |   /-- Positivity: if F ≥ 0 pointwise, then ⟨F⟩ ≥ 0. -/\n1476 |   positivity : ∀ (F : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ),\n1477 |     (∀ U, F U ≥ 0) → evaluate F ≥ 0\n1478 |   /-- Reflection positivity: ⟨f · θ(f)⟩ ≥ 0. -/\n1479 |   reflectionPositive : ∀ (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ),\n1480 |     evaluate (λ U => f U * reflectObservable N f U) ≥ 0\n1481 | \n1482 | /--\n1483 | Construct a `PeriodicExpectation` for the Wilson action on a finite periodic lattice.\n1484 | This uses the Osterwalder-Seiler factorization to prove reflection positivity without\n1485 | the `hadd` limitation of the Z4Site version.\n1486 | -/\n1487 | noncomputable def wilsonPeriodicExpectation (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T) :\n1488 |     PeriodicExpectation N T L β hT :=\n1489 |   {\n1490 |     partitionFunction := partitionFunctionFinite N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n1491 |     partitionFunctionPos := partitionFunctionFinite_pos N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n1492 |     evaluate := gibbsExpectation N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n1493 |     linearity := λ a b F G hF hG =>\n1494 |       gibbsExpectation_linear N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) a b F G hF hG\n1495 |     normalization := gibbsExpectation_normalization N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n1496 |     positivity := λ F hF => gibbsExpectation_pos N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) F hF\n1497 |     reflectionPositive := λ f =>\n1498 |       gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive N T L β hT f\n1499 |   }\n\n... 12 more line(s) below (use offset=1500 to continue reading)","timestamp":1783960372,"token_count":4272,"tool_call_id":"call_00_vDUTlZ6rEhRWXR5XMttU0115","full_token_estimate":3104,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":1511,"byte_count":93799,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":19,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":44,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1783960388,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"query\": \"Mathlib4 \\\"Peter-Weyl\\\" representation theory compact Lie group theorem formalization\"}","name":"web_search"},"id":"call_00_wGbjVKvzoYg9D14dXPw91151","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":1223,"reasoning_content":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me update the todo list and decide what to do.\n\nGiven that:\n1. The gap is thoroughly documented\n2. The project is at a natural decision point\n3. Looking at the code, the `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` lemma is the key missing piece\n\nI have several options. Let me think about which is most productive.\n\nLooking at the code, there's an important thing I notice: the file has `set_option maxHeartbeats 2000000` globally, and this is a very large file (1511 lines). The `end OSDecomposition` at line 1449 closes the section before the `PeriodicExpectation` structure. This means the `sorry` at line 1448 is inside the `OSDecomposition` section.\n\nNow, the `PeriodicExpectation` structure and `wilsonPeriodicExpectation` at lines 1450-1498 DO depend on `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive`, which has the `sorry`. So the entire `PeriodicExpectation` construction depends on closing this gap.\n\nBut the rest of the project (Lattice.lean, SpecialUnitary.lean, LatticeMeasure.lean) does NOT depend on this result, so they would compile fine.\n\nI think the most productive thing I can do now is to create a detailed plan for closing the gap, including a proof sketch, and potentially start writing some infrastructure code.\n\nLet me create a new documentation file that lays out the path forward in detail.\n\nActually, I think the most impactful thing is to create a detailed \"TransferMatrix.lean\" file that sets up the transfer matrix framework, even though it can't be completed without the Peter-Weyl theorem. This would:\n1. Define the transfer matrix as an operator on L²\n2. Prove the identity ∫ G·θG dμ₀ = ⟨G⁺, T G⁺⟩\n3. State the positivity condition as a theorem with the Peter-Weyl assumption\n4. Show how the remaining `sorry` follows from this\n\nThis would be a concrete step forward that structures the remaining work.\n\nBut wait — is this really the most useful thing? Let me reconsider.\n\nThe project can't be built/tested without Lean. So writing more Lean code that can't be tested is risky. Maybe the best thing is to focus on documentation and planning.\n\nLet me create a comprehensive gap analysis document and roadmap, then update the project_task_list and hand off.\n\nActually, I realize I should check what the user actually asked me to do. The user sent me a detailed \"session briefing\" which describes what was completed in the previous session and asks me to continue. The briefing mentions three options:\n\na. Formalizing the Peter-Weyl theorem for SU(N)\nb. Finding a simpler argument specific to the finite lattice case\nc. Leaving the gap as documented and moving to Phase 7\n\nLet me think about each option concretely:\n\n**Option a: Peter-Weyl theorem for SU(N)**\nThis requires formalizing:\n- Representation theory of compact groups (irreps, unitary irreps)\n- The Peter-Weyl theorem: L²(G) ≅ ⊕ V_λ ⊗ V_λ^*\n- Positive-definite functions on compact groups\n- Application to the transfer matrix\n\nThis is a major undertaking (probably months of work for a skilled formalizer). It overlaps with ongoing Mathlib development.\n\n**Option b: Simpler argument for finite lattice**\nThe finite lattice case might admit a simpler proof because:\n- The configuration space is a FINITE product of compact groups\n- The Wilson action is a local function with finite range interaction\n- One could potentially use a \"cluster expansion\" or \"high-temperature expansion\" argument\n\nBut the simplicity is deceptive. Even for finite lattices, the positivity of the transfer matrix requires non-trivial harmonic analysis on SU(N).\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. For a finite lattice, the L² space is still infinite-dimensional (each link variable lives in SU(N), which is a continuous group). However, the Boltzmann factor exp(-β S_W) is a continuous function on a compact space, so the transfer matrix is a compact operator.\n\nFor the Wilson action, there's a known expansion (the \"character expansion\" or \"Fourier expansion\"):\n\nexp(β/N Re Tr(g)) = ∑_{λ} a_λ(β) χ_λ(g)\n\nwhere λ runs over irreducible representations of SU(N), χ_λ is the character, and a_λ(β) ≥ 0 for β > 0 (this is the \"Lee-Yang\" type property).\n\nThen the interface kernel K(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = exp(-β S_OS_int) can be expanded as a product of such character expansions, and the positivity follows from the orthogonality relations of characters (which are part of the Peter-Weyl theorem).\n\nSo actually, the simplest approach is the Peter-Weyl theorem + character expansion. There's no shortcut.\n\n**Option c: Move to Phase 7**\nThis would involve starting on the continuum limit, OS reconstruction, or mass gap.\n\nGiven that:\n1. The Peter-Weyl theorem is the right solution\n2. It's a substantial formalization project\n3. Phase 7 is also substantial\n\nI think the best strategy is:\n1. Start developing the Peter-Weyl theorem infrastructure (this IS Phase 6 completion)\n2. Create the structure for the transfer matrix approach\n3. Document what's needed from Mathlib\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time. Let me search for what Mathlib already has on representation theory of compact groups.","turn":20,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":45,"role":"Tool","content":"Search results (5):\n\n1. [https://dblim.github.io/files/peterweyl_edit.pdf]\n   PDF THE PETER-WEYL THEOREM - GitHub Pages\n   Given a compact Lie group G, there is a bijective correspondence between irreducible, nite-dimensional complex representations of G and dominant integral elements of the weight lattice. The hardest part in the proof the theorem is the construction of an irreducible representation corresponding to some dominant integral element.\n\n2. [https://web.math.princeton.edu/~shouwu/publications/Note23.pdf]\n   PDF Lectures on Representation Theory\n   1 Representations of compact groups In this section, for a compact group G, we prove the Peter-Weyl theorem about the de-composition of L2(G) into a topological sum of irreducible finite-dimensional representations of G × G. We start with basic definitions and examples, followed by the character theory, the Peter-Weyl theorem for finite groups. Then we introduce the Haar measures, matrix ...\n\n3. [https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-755-lie-groups-and-lie-algebras-ii-spring-2024/mit18_755_s24_lec10.pdf]\n   PDF 18.755 S24 Lecture 10: Proof of the Peter-Weyl Theorem\n   36. Proof of the Peter-Weyl theorem 36.1. Compact operators and the Hilbert-Schmidt theorem. To prove the Peter-Weyl theorem, we will use the Hilbert-Schmidt the-orem { the spectral theorem for compact self-adjoint operators in a Hilbert space.\n\n4. [https://www.math.columbia.edu/~woit/LieGroups-2012/peterweyl.pdf]\n   PDF Harmonic Analysis on Compact Lie Groups: the Peter-Weyl Theorem\n   For compact Lie groups, the analytic issues are easily dealt with, but become much trickier in the non-compact case. The Peter-Weyl theorem says that representations of compact Lie groups behave very much like representa-tions of nite groups, with the analytic issues similar to those that occur for Fourier series.\n\n5. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter%E2%80%93Weyl_theorem]\n   Peter-Weyl theorem - Wikipedia\n   Peter-Weyl theorem In mathematics, the Peter-Weyl theorem is a basic result in the theory of harmonic analysis, applying to topological groups that are compact, but are not necessarily abelian. It was initially proved by Hermann Weyl, with his student Fritz Peter, in the setting of a compact topological group G (Peter & Weyl 1927).\n\n","timestamp":1783960389,"token_count":572,"tool_call_id":"call_00_wGbjVKvzoYg9D14dXPw91151","full_token_estimate":531,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"web_search","file_path":null,"old_text":"Mathlib4 \"Peter-Weyl\" representation theory compact Lie group theorem formalization","new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":2276,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":1068,"edit_line":null},"turn":20,"is_prune_marker":false}
