{"id":54,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1783960459,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"content\": \"/-\\n# Transfer Matrix Positivity: Proof Sketch\\n\\nThis file provides a structured proof sketch for the remaining `sorry` in\\n`gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive`. It defines the transfer matrix\\nframework and shows how the integral ∫ G·θG dμ₀ reduces to an inner product\\n⟨G⁺, T G⁺⟩. The final positivity depends on the Peter-Weyl theorem.\\n\\n## Structure\\n\\n1. Partition the link index set into positive, negative, and interface parts\\n2. Factorize the product Haar measure accordingly\\n3. Define the transfer matrix T as an integral operator\\n4. Prove the identity: ∫ G·θG dμ₀ = ⟨G⁺, T G⁺⟩\\n5. State the positivity condition (requires Peter-Weyl)\\n\\n## Status\\n\\nAll steps except the final positivity are provable with current Mathlib.\\nStep 5 requires the Peter-Weyl theorem on SU(N) (or equivalent representation\\ntheory infrastructure).\\n-/\\n\\nimport YangMills.Proofs.ReflectionPositivity\\n\\nopen Set\\nopen Matrix\\nopen scoped BigOperators\\nopen MeasureTheory\\n\\nnamespace YangMills\\nnamespace Lattice\\n\\nsection TransferMatrix\\n\\n/-!\\n### Link Partition\\n\\nOn a periodic lattice `PeriodicSite T L` with odd T, we partition the\\nset of all links (site, μ) into three disjoint subsets:\\n- `positiveLinks`: links at sites with `signedTime > 0`\\n- `negativeLinks`: links at sites with `signedTime < 0`\\n- `interfaceLinks`: links at sites with `signedTime = 0`\\n\\nThis partition is induced by the time-coordinate of the base site.\\n-/\\n\\nvariable (N T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T) (β : ℝ)\\n\\n/-- The set of links at positive-time sites (signed time > 0). -/\\nnoncomputable def positiveLinks : Finset (PeriodicSite T L × Fin 4) :=\\n  (positiveSites T L).product (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin 4))\\n\\n/-- The set of links at negative-time sites (signed time < 0). -/\\nnoncomputable def negativeLinks : Finset (PeriodicSite T L × Fin 4) :=\\n  (negativeSites T L).product (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin 4))\\n\\n/-- The set of links at interface sites (signed time = 0). -/\\nnoncomputable def interfaceLinks : Finset (PeriodicSite T L × Fin 4) :=\\n  (interfaceSites T L).product (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin 4))\\n\\nlemma linkPartition_disjoint_union :\\n    positiveLinks N T L ∪ negativeLinks N T L ∪ interfaceLinks N T L =\\n    (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L × Fin 4)) := by\\n  ext ⟨n, μ⟩; simp [positiveLinks, negativeLinks, interfaceLinks, positiveSites,\\n    negativeSites, interfaceSites]\\n  -- Every site has signed time > 0, < 0, or = 0, so every link belongs to one of the three sets\\n  by_cases hpos : signedTime T n.time > 0\\n  · simp [hpos]\\n  · by_cases hneg : signedTime T n.time < 0\\n    · simp [hpos, hneg]\\n    · have hzero : signedTime T n.time = 0 := by\\n        have hle : signedTime T n.time ≤ 0 := by linarith\\n        have hge : signedTime T n.time ≥ 0 := by linarith\\n        linarith\\n      simp [hpos, hneg, hzero]\\n\\nlemma linkPartition_disjoint : \\n    Disjoint (positiveLinks N T L) (negativeLinks N T L) ∧\\n    Disjoint (positiveLinks N T L) (interfaceLinks N T L) ∧\\n    Disjoint (negativeLinks N T L) (interfaceLinks N T L) := by\\n  -- Follows from disjointness of the site partitions\\n  have h_pos_neg : Disjoint (positiveSites T L) (negativeSites T L) := by\\n    unfold positiveSites negativeSites\\n    rw [Finset.disjoint_filter]\\n    intro n hn hpos hneg; linarith\\n  have h_pos_int : Disjoint (positiveSites T L) (interfaceSites T L) := by\\n    unfold positiveSites interfaceSites\\n    rw [Finset.disjoint_filter]\\n    intro n hn hpos hzero; linarith\\n  have h_neg_int : Disjoint (negativeSites T L) (interfaceSites T L) := by\\n    unfold negativeSites interfaceSites\\n    rw [Finset.disjoint_filter]\\n    intro n hn hneg hzero; linarith\\n  refine ⟨?_, ?_, ?_⟩\\n  · exact Finset.disjoint_product.mpr ⟨h_pos_neg, λ _ => by simp⟩\\n  · exact Finset.disjoint_product.mpr ⟨h_pos_int, λ _ => by simp⟩\\n  · exact Finset.disjoint_product.mpr ⟨h_neg_int, λ _ => by simp⟩\\n\\n/-!\\n### Measure Factorization\\n\\nThe product Haar measure factorizes as μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰.\\n-/\\n\\n/-- Haar measure on the positive links. -/\\nnoncomputable def haarMeasurePositive : Measure (FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) :=\\n  productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (positiveSites T L)\\n\\n/-- Haar measure on the negative links. -/\\nnoncomputable def haarMeasureNegative : Measure (FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) :=\\n  productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (negativeSites T L)\\n\\n/-- Haar measure on the interface links. -/\\nnoncomputable def haarMeasureInterface : Measure (FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) :=\\n  productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (interfaceSites T L)\\n\\n-- The measure factorization theorem: μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰ under the link partition.\\n-- This follows from the general fact that product measures factorize over disjoint unions\\n-- of the index set.\\n\\nlemma measure_factorization :\\n    productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) =\\n    haarMeasurePositive N T L ⊗ₘ haarMeasureNegative N T L ⊗ₘ haarMeasureInterface N T L := by\\n  -- This requires identifying FiniteLinkConfig N PeriodicSite (Finset.univ) with\\n  -- the product of configurations on positiveSites, negativeSites, and interfaceSites.\\n  -- The bijection is given by restricting and recombining.\\n  -- This is a standard result about product measures on disjoint unions of index sets.\\n  -- TODO: formalize once the necessary measure-theoretic infrastructure is in place.\\n  sorry\\n\\n/-!\\n### Transfer Matrix Definition\\n\\nThe transfer matrix T acts on functions on the positive-and-interface configuration space.\\n-/\\n\\n/--\\nThe configuration space for positive and interface links together.\\n-/\\nabbrev PosInterfaceConfig :=\\n  FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (positiveSites T L ∪ interfaceSites T L)\\n\\n/--\\nThe restriction of a full configuration to the positive and interface links.\\n-/\\nnoncomputable def restrictPosInterface (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) :\\n    PosInterfaceConfig N T L :=\\n  λ idx => cfg ⟨(idx.1.1, idx.1.2), by\\n    rcases idx with ⟨(n, μ), hn⟩\\n    -- If n ∈ positiveSites ∪ interfaceSites, then (n, μ) is valid in the full config\\n    -- We need to show the restriction is well-defined\\n    -- This requires that positiveLinks ∪ interfaceLinks ⊆ univ, which is true.\\n    exact Finset.mem_univ (n, μ)⟩\\n\\n/--\\nThe transfer matrix T acting on L² functions of the positive+interface configuration. \\nFor ψ depending on U⁺ (positive links) and U⁰ (interface links),\\n\\n  (Tψ)(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ ψ(U⁻, U⁰) · exp(-β · S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\\n\\nwhere the integral is over the negative-link configurations.\\n-/\\nnoncomputable def transferMatrix (ψ : PosInterfaceConfig N T L → ℝ) :\\n    PosInterfaceConfig N T L → ℝ :=\\n  λ (U_plus_zero : PosInterfaceConfig N T L) =>\\n    ∫ (U_minus : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (negativeSites T L)),\\n      ψ (mergeConfigurations N T L U_minus U_plus_zero) *\\n      Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β\\n        (extendToFullConfig N T L U_minus U_plus_zero))\\n    ∂ haarMeasureNegative N T L\\n\\n/--\\nMerging a negative configuration and a positive+interface configuration into a full config.\\nThis requires a bijection between the full index set and the disjoint union of the three parts.\\n-/\\nnoncomputable def mergeConfigurations\\n    (U_minus : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (negativeSites T L))\\n    (U_plus_zero : PosInterfaceConfig N T L) :\\n    FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) :=\\n  λ idx => \\n    -- Determine which part the index belongs to and use the appropriate config\\n    -- This requires a case split on whether idx's site is in positive, negative, or interface\\n    -- TODO: implement using the link partition\\n    sorry\\n\\n/--\\nExtend a configuration on positive+interface+negative to a full link variable.\\n-/\\nnoncomputable def extendToFullConfig\\n    (U_minus : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (negativeSites T L))\\n    (U_plus_zero : PosInterfaceConfig N T L) :\\n    LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) :=\\n  extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\\n    (mergeConfigurations N T L U_minus U_plus_zero)\\n\\n/-!\\n### Key Identity\\n\\nThe main identity relating the integral of G·θG to the inner product ⟨G⁺, T G⁺⟩.\\n-/\\n\\nsection KeyIdentity\\n\\nvariable (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ)\\n\\n-- The function G as defined in the reflection positivity proof\\nset G := λ (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) =>\\n  f U * Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β U) *\\n  Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U / 2) with hG\\n\\n/--\\nThe restriction of G to the positive+interface configuration space.\\n-/\\nnoncomputable def G_plus (cfg : PosInterfaceConfig N T L) : ℝ :=\\n  G (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (positiveSites T L ∪ interfaceSites T L) cfg)\\n\\nlemma transferMatrix_identity :\\n    (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\\n      G (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg) *\\n      G (reflectLinkVariable N (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg))\\n      ∂ productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =\\n    (∫ (cfg : PosInterfaceConfig N T L),\\n      G_plus N T L β f cfg * (transferMatrix N T L β (G_plus N T L β f) cfg)\\n      ∂ (haarMeasurePositive N T L ⊗ₘ haarMeasureInterface N T L)) := by\\n  -- This identity follows from:\\n  -- 1. Measure factorization (μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰)\\n  -- 2. The reflection maps negative links to positive links bijectively\\n  -- 3. Fubini's theorem to separate the integrals\\n  -- 4. The definition of the transfer matrix\\n  -- \\n  -- The full proof requires substantial measure-theoretic infrastructure.\\n  -- Once the factorization and merge operations are properly defined,\\n  -- this becomes a direct computation.\\n  sorry\\n\\nend KeyIdentity\\n\\n/-!\\n### Positivity of the Transfer Matrix\\n\\nThis is the core analytic fact. It requires the Peter-Weyl theorem\\n(or equivalent representation theory).\\n-/\\n\\n/--\\nThe interface Boltzmann kernel K(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = exp(-β · S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)).\\n-/\\nnoncomputable def interfaceKernel\\n    (U_plus_zero : PosInterfaceConfig N T L)\\n    (U_minus : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (negativeSites T L)) : ℝ :=\\n  Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β\\n    (extendToFullConfig N T L U_minus U_plus_zero))\\n\\n/--\\n**Positive-definite kernel property (Peter-Weyl)**:\\n\\nFor any finite set of configurations (U⁺_i, U⁰_i) and coefficients c_i ∈ ℝ,\\n  ∑_{i,j} c_i c_j · ∫ K(U⁺_i, U⁻, U⁰_i) K(U⁺_j, U⁻, U⁰_j) dμ⁻(U⁻) ≥ 0\\n\\nThis is equivalent to the positivity of the transfer matrix T,\\ni.e., ⟨ψ, Tψ⟩ ≥ 0 for all ψ.\\n\\nProof sketch:\\n1. The interface action S_OS_int is a sum over interface plaquettes.\\n2. Each interface plaquette term is of the form exp(β/N Re Tr(plaquette_product)).\\n3. On SU(N), the function g ↦ exp(c Re Tr(g)) is positive-definite for c > 0.\\n   This follows from the Peter-Weyl theorem: it expands in characters with\\n   non-negative coefficients.\\n4. Products of positive-definite functions are positive-definite.\\n5. Therefore K is positive-definite, and T is positive.\\n-/\\nlemma transferMatrix_positive (ψ : PosInterfaceConfig N T L → ℝ) :\\n    0 ≤ (∫ (cfg : PosInterfaceConfig N T L),\\n      ψ cfg * (transferMatrix N T L β ψ) cfg\\n      ∂ (haarMeasurePositive N T L ⊗ₘ haarMeasureInterface N T L)) := by\\n  -- This is the statement that ⟨ψ, Tψ⟩ ≥ 0.\\n  -- It requires the Peter-Weyl theorem on SU(N).\\n  -- See docs/gap_analysis.md for full discussion.\\n  sorry\\n\\n/--\\nCombining the key identity with the positivity of the transfer matrix,\\nwe obtain the desired result:\\n\\n  ∫ G(U) · G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\\n-/\\nlemma integral_G_thetaG_nonneg (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ) :\\n    0 ≤ (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\\n      G N T L β f (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg) *\\n      G N T L β f (reflectLinkVariable N (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg))\\n      ∂ productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) := by\\n  rw [transferMatrix_identity N T L β f]\\n  apply transferMatrix_positive N T L β\\n\\nend TransferMatrix\\n\\nend Lattice\\nend YangMills\\n\", \"path\": \"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\"}","name":"write_file"},"id":"call_00_65o5vUKENteSinPXjoXQ4226","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":2984,"reasoning_content":"Good. Now let me create a proof sketch for the transfer matrix approach — a Lean code sketch showing what the proof WOULD look like when the infrastructure is available.","turn":25,"is_prune_marker":false}
